\date{2026-09-05}
\title{Convex subcubes and the clamping retraction}
\author{ulrikbuchholtz}
\taxon{Module}
\import{stt-macros}

\p{This module proves that [the ordered subcube](ub-000Y) includes inner
anodynely into the cube in every dimension (\ref{ub-0030}), by adjoining the
comparability of one pair of coordinates at a time. Write #{\square^n_I} for
the subtype of the cube cut out by comparability of the pairs in a list #{I},
so that #{\square^n_{[]}} is the cube and #{\square^n_{\text{all}}} is
#{\mathrm{Ord}_n}, and call a subtype of the cube \em{convex} when it is a
conjunction of inequalities #{x_a \le x_b} between coordinates. There are three
steps.}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The convex case.} For convex #{A} and any pair #{(i,j)},
#{A \land \square^n_{(i,j)} \hookrightarrow A} is inner anodyne: this is
\ref{ub-002M} when #{A} leaves the pair unrelated, and otherwise the
inclusion is an equivalence (\ref{ub-002W}).}
\li{\strong{Off the convex case.} Hence the same for every subtype that is a
finite union of convex ones, by [the glueing lemma](ub-000T).}
\li{\strong{The conclusion.} Adjoining the pairs of #{I} one at a time, each
step is step 2, and inner anodyne maps compose (\ref{ub-002Y}).}
}

\p{The module is organised accordingly.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The retraction} (\ref{ub-002C} to \ref{ub-002F}): slots, convex
presentations, and the clamp that retracts the cube onto a convex subcube.}
\li{\strong{The convex case} (\ref{ub-002G} to \ref{ub-002N}): first for the
last two coordinates, then for any unrelated pair by reindexing.}
\li{\strong{Presentations from lists} (\ref{ub-002P} to \ref{ub-002V}):
adjoining one relation to a closed presentation keeps it closed, so a list of
relations names a convex subcube and concatenation is the meet.}
\li{\strong{Steps 2 and 3} (\ref{ub-002W} to \ref{ub-002Z}): the convex case
for every list, the recursion over a list of pairs, and the enumeration of
all pairs.}
}

\p{Step 1 rests on a retraction of the cube onto a convex subtype that fixes
the two coordinates being compared. It is from \citek{SGWB26}, based on
initial ideas by Gratzer and Schellingerhout.}

\p{The construction is a \em{clamp}. A convex subtype of #{\square^n} is cut
out by inequalities #{x_a \le x_b} between coordinates; to retract the cube
onto it, run through the coordinates and, at each one, push every earlier
coordinate to the correct side of it — meeting with it below, joining with it
above, leaving it alone when the two are unrelated. Nothing is done to the
coordinate being processed, so the last coordinate is untouched and the
second-to-last is untouched exactly when the two are unrelated. That is what
makes the retraction fix an incomparable pair, and it is why the comparability
of that pair transfers from the cube to the subtype.}

\p{The bookkeeping is a labelling of each coordinate by an element of
#{\{\le, =, \parallel, \ge\}}, and the whole of the case analysis in
\citek{SGWB26} collapses to one fact: the clamp is monotone in the label and the
value jointly, for the order #{\le \; < \; \{=, \parallel\} \; < \; \ge}. The
side conditions on a presentation are then exactly that each labelling is
monotone for the ones below it.}

\agda{
open import Foundations.Universes
open import Algebra.Lattice

module Synthetic.Categories.ConvexSubcubes
  {𝓘} (Δ¹ : Type 𝓘) (I : Lattice Δ¹) where

open Lattice I renaming (0l to i0; 1l to i1)

open import Axioms.UF
}

\agda-imports{
\agda{
open import Foundations.Prelude
open import Foundations.Fin
open import Foundations.List

open import Core.Arrows
open import Core.ArrowEquiv
open import Core.ArrowRetracts
open import Core.Joins
open import Core.SubtypeUnions
open import Core.Orthogonal
open import Core.OrthogonalClosure

open import Synthetic.Cubes Δ¹ i0 i1
open import Synthetic.Simplicial Δ¹ I
open import Synthetic.Horns Δ¹ I
open import Synthetic.Categories.Precategories Δ¹ I
open import Synthetic.Categories.SquareLifting Δ¹ I
}
}

\subtree[ub-002C]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Slots, and the clamp of one coordinate}

\p{A \em{slot} records how one coordinate is constrained relative to another:
below it, equal to it, unrelated to it, or above it. The clamp of #{x} at #{t}
in a slot moves #{x} to the side of #{t} that the slot asks for, and does the
least it can:}

##{\mathrm{cl}_{\le}(t,x) = t \wedge x,\quad \mathrm{cl}_{=}(t,x) = t,\quad
   \mathrm{cl}_{\parallel}(t,x) = x,\quad \mathrm{cl}_{\ge}(t,x) = t \vee x.}

\agda{
data Slot : Type where
  lo eq fr hi : Slot

□-clamp¹ : Slot → Δ¹ → Δ¹ → Δ¹
□-clamp¹ lo t x = t ∧ x
□-clamp¹ eq t x = t
□-clamp¹ fr t x = x
□-clamp¹ hi t x = t ∨ x

bound¹ : Slot → Δ¹ → Δ¹ → Type 𝓘
bound¹ lo x t = x ≤ t
bound¹ eq x t = x ＝ t
bound¹ fr x t = 𝟙ᴸ
bound¹ hi x t = t ≤ x

bound¹-is-prop : (s : Slot) (x t : Δ¹) → is-prop (bound¹ s x t)
bound¹-is-prop lo x t = ≤-is-prop
bound¹-is-prop eq x t = carrier-is-set _ _
bound¹-is-prop fr x t = is-prop←is-single 𝟙ᴸ-is-singleton
bound¹-is-prop hi x t = ≤-is-prop
}

\p{The clamp does what it was asked, and it does nothing when there was
nothing to do.}

\agda{
□-clamp¹-self : (s : Slot) (t x : Δ¹) → bound¹ s (□-clamp¹ s t x) t
□-clamp¹-self lo t x = ∧-≤-eliml ≤-refl
□-clamp¹-self eq t x = refl
□-clamp¹-self fr t x = ttᴸ
□-clamp¹-self hi t x = ∨-≤-introl ≤-refl

□-clamp¹-fix : (s : Slot) (t x : Δ¹) → bound¹ s x t → □-clamp¹ s t x ＝ x
□-clamp¹-fix lo t x p = ∧-comm ∙ p
□-clamp¹-fix eq t x p = sym p
□-clamp¹-fix fr t x p = refl
□-clamp¹-fix hi t x p = ≤-max p
}

\p{The order on slots is the one that makes the clamp monotone:
#{\le \; \sqsubset \; = \; \sqsubset \; \ge} and
#{\le \; \sqsubset \; \parallel \; \sqsubset \; \ge}, with #{=} and
#{\parallel} incomparable. Everything below rests on the single lemma that
follows it.}

\agda{
_⊑_ : Slot → Slot → Type
lo ⊑ _ = 𝟙
eq ⊑ eq = 𝟙
eq ⊑ hi = 𝟙
eq ⊑ _ = ∅
fr ⊑ fr = 𝟙
fr ⊑ hi = 𝟙
fr ⊑ _ = ∅
hi ⊑ hi = 𝟙
hi ⊑ _ = ∅

□-clamp¹-mono
  : (p q : Slot) → p ⊑ q → (t : Δ¹) {x y : Δ¹} → x ≤ y
  → □-clamp¹ p t x ≤ □-clamp¹ q t y
□-clamp¹-mono lo lo _ t h = ∧-UP (∧-≤-eliml ≤-refl , ∧-≤-elimr h)
□-clamp¹-mono lo eq _ t h = ∧-≤-eliml ≤-refl
□-clamp¹-mono lo fr _ t h = ∧-≤-elimr h
□-clamp¹-mono lo hi _ t h = ≤-trans (∧-≤-eliml ≤-refl) (∨-≤-introl ≤-refl)
□-clamp¹-mono eq eq _ t h = ≤-refl
□-clamp¹-mono eq hi _ t h = ∨-≤-introl ≤-refl
□-clamp¹-mono fr fr _ t h = h
□-clamp¹-mono fr hi _ t h = ∨-≤-intror h
□-clamp¹-mono hi hi _ t h = ∨-UP (∨-≤-introl ≤-refl , ∨-≤-intror h)
}
}

\subtree[ub-002D]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Convex presentations}

\p{A \em{labelling} of the #{n} coordinates of #{\square^n} assigns a slot to
each. A \em{convex presentation} of dimension #{n} is a labelling for every
coordinate, saying how it is constrained relative to the coordinates before
it: a triangular matrix of slots, listed one row at a time. It cuts out the
subtype on which every one of those constraints holds.}

\agda{
Lab : ℕ → Type
Lab zero = 𝟙
Lab (suc n) = Lab n × Slot

Cvx : ℕ → Type
Cvx zero = 𝟙
Cvx (suc n) = Cvx n × Lab n

Bound : ∀ {n} → Lab n → □^ n → Δ¹ → Type 𝓘
Bound {zero} _ _ _ = 𝟙ᴸ
Bound {suc zero} (_ , s) x t = bound¹ s x t
Bound {suc (suc n)} (ℓ , s) (w , x) t = Bound ℓ w t × bound¹ s x t

Locus : ∀ {n} → Cvx n → □^ n → Type 𝓘
Locus {zero} _ _ = 𝟙ᴸ
Locus {suc zero} _ _ = 𝟙ᴸ
Locus {suc (suc n)} (Ψ , ℓ) (w , t) = Locus Ψ w × Bound ℓ w t
}

\agda{
Bound-is-prop : ∀ {n} (ℓ : Lab n) (v : □^ n) (t : Δ¹) → is-prop (Bound ℓ v t)
Bound-is-prop {zero} _ _ _ = is-prop←is-single 𝟙ᴸ-is-singleton
Bound-is-prop {suc zero} (_ , s) x t = bound¹-is-prop s x t
Bound-is-prop {suc (suc n)} (ℓ , s) (w , x) t
  = ×-is-prop (Bound-is-prop ℓ w t) (bound¹-is-prop s x t)

Locus-is-prop : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (v : □^ n) → is-prop (Locus Ψ v)
Locus-is-prop {zero} _ _ = is-prop←is-single 𝟙ᴸ-is-singleton
Locus-is-prop {suc zero} _ _ = is-prop←is-single 𝟙ᴸ-is-singleton
Locus-is-prop {suc (suc n)} (Ψ , ℓ) (w , t)
  = ×-is-prop (Locus-is-prop Ψ w) (Bound-is-prop ℓ w t)
}

\p{Not every triangular matrix of slots is a presentation one can retract
onto: the rows have to fit together. The condition is that each labelling is
\em{monotone} for the constraints already declared — if coordinate #{a} is
declared below coordinate #{b}, then #{a}'s slot in a later row must be
#{\sqsubseteq} #{b}'s. In the intended reading, where the presentation is a
preorder on the coordinates, this is transitivity; here it is exactly the
hypothesis [the clamp lemma](ub-002C) wants, and nothing more is assumed.}

\agda{
compat¹ : Slot → Slot → Slot → Type
compat¹ lo b s = b ⊑ s
compat¹ eq b s = (b ⊑ s) × (s ⊑ b)
compat¹ fr b s = 𝟙
compat¹ hi b s = s ⊑ b

Compat : ∀ {n} → Lab n → Lab n → Slot → Type
Compat {zero} _ _ _ = 𝟙
Compat {suc n} (ℓ , a) (τ , b) s = Compat ℓ τ s × compat¹ a b s

Mono : ∀ {n} → Cvx n → Lab n → Type
Mono {zero} _ _ = 𝟙
Mono {suc n} (Ψ , ℓ) (τ , s) = Mono Ψ τ × Compat ℓ τ s

is-coherent : ∀ {n} → Cvx n → Type
is-coherent {zero} _ = 𝟙
is-coherent {suc n} (Ψ , ℓ) = is-coherent Ψ × Mono Ψ ℓ
}
}

\subtree[ub-002E]{
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Clamping preserves the locus}

\p{Clamping every coordinate of the cube at a single #{t}, in the slots of a
labelling #{\rho}, sends the locus of #{\Psi} into itself as soon as #{\rho}
is monotone for #{\Psi}. This is the whole of the case analysis in the proof
of \citet{Lem. 3.1}{SGWB26}, and it is one appeal to
[the clamp lemma](ub-002C) per constraint.}

\agda{
□-clamp : ∀ {n} → Lab n → Δ¹ → □^ n → □^ n
□-clamp {zero} _ t v = v
□-clamp {suc zero} (_ , s) t x = □-clamp¹ s t x
□-clamp {suc (suc n)} (ℓ , s) t (w , x) = (□-clamp ℓ t w , □-clamp¹ s t x)
}

\p{First the scalar step: one declared constraint, clamped on both sides.}

\agda{
bound¹-clamp
  : (a b s : Slot) → compat¹ a b s → (t x u : Δ¹) → bound¹ a x u
  → bound¹ a (□-clamp¹ b t x) (□-clamp¹ s t u)
bound¹-clamp lo b s c t x u p = □-clamp¹-mono b s c t p
bound¹-clamp eq b s c t x u p
  = ≤-antisym (□-clamp¹-mono b s (c .fst) t (≤←＝ p))
              (□-clamp¹-mono s b (c .snd) t (≥←＝ p))
bound¹-clamp fr b s c t x u p = ttᴸ
bound¹-clamp hi b s c t x u p = □-clamp¹-mono s b c t p
}

\p{Then the two inductions: a whole row of constraints, and the whole
presentation.}

\agda{
Bound-clamp
  : ∀ {n} (ℓ τ : Lab n) (s : Slot) → Compat ℓ τ s
  → (t : Δ¹) (w : □^ n) (u : Δ¹) → Bound ℓ w u
  → Bound ℓ (□-clamp τ t w) (□-clamp¹ s t u)
Bound-clamp {zero} _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ = ttᴸ
Bound-clamp {suc zero} (_ , a) (_ , b) s (_ , c) t x u p
  = bound¹-clamp a b s c t x u p
Bound-clamp {suc (suc n)} (ℓ , a) (τ , b) s (cs , c) t (w , x) u (ps , p)
  = ( Bound-clamp ℓ τ s cs t w u ps
    , bound¹-clamp a b s c t x u p )

Locus-clamp
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (ρ : Lab n) → Mono Ψ ρ
  → (t : Δ¹) (v : □^ n) → Locus Ψ v → Locus Ψ (□-clamp ρ t v)
Locus-clamp {zero} _ _ _ _ _ _ = ttᴸ
Locus-clamp {suc zero} _ _ _ _ _ _ = ttᴸ
Locus-clamp {suc (suc n)} (Ψ , ℓ) (τ , s) (m , c) t (w , u) (lw , bd)
  = ( Locus-clamp Ψ τ m t w lw
    , Bound-clamp ℓ τ s c t w u bd )
}

\p{The two facts about a single row that the retraction needs: the clamped
point satisfies the row it was clamped by, and clamping changes nothing on a
point that already satisfies it.}

\agda{
Bound-clamp-self
  : ∀ {n} (ρ : Lab n) (t : Δ¹) (v : □^ n) → Bound ρ (□-clamp ρ t v) t
Bound-clamp-self {zero} _ t v = ttᴸ
Bound-clamp-self {suc zero} (_ , s) t x = □-clamp¹-self s t x
Bound-clamp-self {suc (suc n)} (ℓ , s) t (w , x)
  = (Bound-clamp-self ℓ t w , □-clamp¹-self s t x)

□-clamp-fix
  : ∀ {n} (ρ : Lab n) (t : Δ¹) (v : □^ n) → Bound ρ v t → □-clamp ρ t v ＝ v
□-clamp-fix {zero} _ t v p = refl
□-clamp-fix {suc zero} (_ , s) t x p = □-clamp¹-fix s t x p
□-clamp-fix {suc (suc n)} (ℓ , s) t (w , x) (ps , p)
  = ×-path→ (□-clamp-fix ℓ t w ps , □-clamp¹-fix s t x p)
}
}

\subtree[ub-002F]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The retraction onto a convex subcube}

\p{Every coherent convex subcube is a retract of its cube. The retraction
processes the coordinates from the last inwards: at each step the coordinate
itself is kept, and everything before it is clamped at its value.}

\agda{
□-retract : ∀ {n} → Cvx n → □^ n → □^ n
□-retract {zero} _ v = v
□-retract {suc zero} _ v = v
□-retract {suc (suc n)} (Ψ , ℓ) (w , t) = (□-clamp ℓ t (□-retract Ψ w) , t)

□-retract-Locus
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) → is-coherent Ψ → (v : □^ n) → Locus Ψ (□-retract Ψ v)
□-retract-Locus {zero} _ _ _ = ttᴸ
□-retract-Locus {suc zero} _ _ _ = ttᴸ
□-retract-Locus {suc (suc n)} (Ψ , ℓ) (c , m) (w , t)
  = ( Locus-clamp Ψ ℓ m t (□-retract Ψ w) (□-retract-Locus Ψ c w)
    , Bound-clamp-self ℓ t (□-retract Ψ w) )

□-retract-fix
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (v : □^ n) → Locus Ψ v → □-retract Ψ v ＝ v
□-retract-fix {zero} _ v p = refl
□-retract-fix {suc zero} _ v p = refl
□-retract-fix {suc (suc n)} (Ψ , ℓ) (w , t) (lw , bd)
  = ×-path→ ( ap (□-clamp ℓ t) (□-retract-fix Ψ w lw) ∙ □-clamp-fix ℓ t w bd
            , refl )
}

\p{What makes the retraction useful is \em{which coordinates it fixes}
(\ref{ub-002I}): the last coordinate is never touched, and the second-to-last
is touched only by the last one's row. That is the conclusion of
\citet{Lem. 3.1}{SGWB26}.}

\agda{
□-last : ∀ {n} → □^ (suc n) → Δ¹
□-last {zero} v = v
□-last {suc n} v = v .snd

□-clamp-last
  : ∀ {n} (ρ : Lab (suc n)) (t : Δ¹) (v : □^ (suc n))
  → □-last (□-clamp ρ t v) ＝ □-clamp¹ (ρ .snd) t (□-last v)
□-clamp-last {zero} _ t v = refl
□-clamp-last {suc n} _ t v = refl

□-retract-last
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx (suc n)) (v : □^ (suc n))
  → □-last (□-retract Ψ v) ＝ □-last v
□-retract-last {zero} _ v = refl
□-retract-last {suc n} _ v = refl
}

\p{So if the top row of the presentation puts the last two coordinates in the
unrelated slot, the retraction fixes them both.}

\agda{
□-last₂ : ∀ {n} → □^ (suc (suc n)) → Δ¹ × Δ¹
□-last₂ v = (□-last (v .fst) , v .snd)

□-retract-last₂
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx (suc n)) (τ : Lab n) (v : □^ (suc (suc n)))
  → □-last₂ (□-retract (Ψ , (τ , fr)) v) ＝ □-last₂ v
□-retract-last₂ Ψ τ (w , t)
  = ×-path→ ( □-clamp-last (τ , fr) t (□-retract Ψ w) ∙ □-retract-last Ψ w
            , refl )
}
}

\subtree[ub-002G]{
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Comparability of the last two coordinates of a cube}

\p{The free case of step 1, in the position the retraction leaves fixed: on
the cube itself, comparability of the last two coordinates is
#{\id \times (S \hookrightarrow \Delta^1\times\Delta^1)} up to the
associativity that splits #{\square^{n+2}} as #{\square^n \times \square^2},
and left orthogonal maps are closed under products with an identity.}

\agda{
□-comparable₂ : ∀ {n} → □^ (suc (suc n)) → Type 𝓘
□-comparable₂ v = generating-fam-is-simplicial (□-last₂ v)

□-join : ∀ {n} → □^ n × (Δ¹ × Δ¹) → □^ (suc (suc n))
□-join {zero} (_ , u) = u
□-join {suc n} (w , u) = ((w , u .fst) , u .snd)

□-split : ∀ {n} → □^ (suc (suc n)) → □^ n × (Δ¹ × Δ¹)
□-split {zero} v = (ttᴸ , v)
□-split {suc n} v = (v .fst .fst , (v .fst .snd , v .snd))

private
  □-join-η : ∀ {n} (x : □^ n × (Δ¹ × Δ¹)) → □-split (□-join x) ＝ x
  □-join-η {zero} _ = refl
  □-join-η {suc n} _ = refl

  □-join-ε : ∀ {n} (v : □^ (suc (suc n))) → □-join (□-split v) ＝ v
  □-join-ε {zero} _ = refl
  □-join-ε {suc n} _ = refl

□-join≃ : ∀ {n} → □^ n × (Δ¹ × Δ¹) ≃ □^ (suc (suc n))
□-join≃ = mk≃ □-join (is-equiv←qinv (□-split , □-join-η , □-join-ε))
}

\agda{
private
  chart-top
    : ∀ {n} → □^ n × Σ (Δ¹ × Δ¹) generating-fam-is-simplicial
    → Σ (□^ (suc (suc n))) (□-comparable₂ {n})
  chart-top {zero} (_ , (u , p)) = (u , p)
  chart-top {suc n} (w , (u , p)) = (((w , u .fst) , u .snd) , p)

  chart-bot
    : ∀ {n} → Σ (□^ (suc (suc n))) (□-comparable₂ {n})
    → □^ n × Σ (Δ¹ × Δ¹) generating-fam-is-simplicial
  chart-bot {zero} (v , p) = (ttᴸ , (v , p))
  chart-bot {suc n} (v , p) = (v .fst .fst , ((v .fst .snd , v .snd) , p))

  chart-η
    : ∀ {n} (x : □^ n × Σ (Δ¹ × Δ¹) generating-fam-is-simplicial)
    → chart-bot (chart-top x) ＝ x
  chart-η {zero} _ = refl
  chart-η {suc n} _ = refl

  chart-ε
    : ∀ {n} (x : Σ (□^ (suc (suc n))) (□-comparable₂ {n}))
    → chart-top (chart-bot x) ＝ x
  chart-ε {zero} _ = refl
  chart-ε {suc n} _ = refl

  chart≃
    : ∀ {n} → □^ n × Σ (Δ¹ × Δ¹) generating-fam-is-simplicial
    ≃ Σ (□^ (suc (suc n))) (□-comparable₂ {n})
  chart≃ = mk≃ chart-top (is-equiv←qinv (chart-bot , chart-η , chart-ε))

  chart-comm
    : ∀ {n} (x : □^ n × Σ (Δ¹ × Δ¹) generating-fam-is-simplicial)
    → □-join ((id ×₁ fst) x) ＝ chart-top x .fst
  chart-comm {zero} _ = refl
  chart-comm {suc n} _ = refl

□-comparable₂-is-inner-anodyne
  : ∀ {n} → is-inner-anodyne (fst {B = □-comparable₂ {n}})
□-comparable₂-is-inner-anodyne {n}
  = is-anodyne←equiv (id ×₁ fst) fst
      (mk-arr-equiv chart≃ □-join≃ chart-comm)
      Λ[2,1]-incl
      (λ g gp → left-orthogonal-× id fst g (id⊥ g)
                  (simplicial-square-incl-is-inner-anodyne g gp))
}
}

\subtree[ub-002H]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Comparability over a convex subcube is inner anodyne}

\p{Step 1 of [the outline](Synthetic.Categories.ConvexSubcubes), for a pair
sitting in the last two coordinates; this is \citet{Lem. 3.3}{SGWB26}. Let
#{\Psi} be a coherent convex presentation of #{\square^{n+2}} whose top row
leaves the last two coordinates unrelated. Then adjoining their comparability
to the locus of #{\Psi} is inner anodyne. (The lemma there concludes the
stronger statement that the inclusion lies in the class generated by the
[triangulated square](stt-00CC); since that map is itself inner anodyne
(\ref{ub-000F}), the class is contained in the inner anodyne maps, and inner
anodyne is all that Segal types need.)}

\p{The proof is [the retract lemma](ub-000S) applied to the square}

\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}
	{A \wedge \square^{n+2}_{(i,j)}} && {\square^{n+2}_{(i,j)}} && {A \wedge \square^{n+2}_{(i,j)}} \\
	\\
	A && {\square^{n+2}} && A
	\arrow["s", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[hook, from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[hook, from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["r", from=1-3, to=1-5]
	\arrow[hook, from=1-5, to=3-5]
	\arrow["s"', from=3-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow["r"', from=3-3, to=3-5]
\end{tikzcd}
}

\p{whose bottom row is the inclusion of the locus followed by
[the retraction](ub-002F), and whose top row is the same maps restricted to
the comparability loci — which they do restrict to, because the retraction
fixes both coordinates. The right-hand column is
[the free case](ub-002G).}

\agda{
cvx-ext : ∀ {n} → Cvx (suc n) → Lab n → Cvx (suc (suc n))
cvx-ext Ψ τ = (Ψ , (τ , fr))

comparable-on
  : ∀ {n} (P : □^ (suc (suc n)) → Type 𝓘) → Σ (□^ (suc (suc n))) P → Type 𝓘
comparable-on P q = □-comparable₂ (q .fst)

module _
  {n : ℕ} (Ψ : Cvx (suc n)) (τ : Lab n)
  (coh : is-coherent Ψ) (mono : Mono Ψ (τ , fr))
  where

  private
    Ψ⁺ : Cvx (suc (suc n))
    Ψ⁺ = cvx-ext Ψ τ

  convex-comparability-is-inner-anodyne
    : is-inner-anodyne (fst {B = comparable-on (Locus Ψ⁺)})
  convex-comparability-is-inner-anodyne
    = is-anodyne←Arrow-retract Λ[2,1]-incl
        (subtype-Arrow-retract
          (λ q → generating-fam-is-simplicial-is-prop (□-last₂ (q .fst)))
          (subtype-is-set (□-is-set carrier-is-set) (Locus-is-prop Ψ⁺))
          fst
          (λ v → (□-retract Ψ⁺ v , □-retract-Locus Ψ⁺ (coh , mono) v))
          (λ q → subtype-ext (Locus-is-prop Ψ⁺)
                   (□-retract-fix Ψ⁺ (q .fst) (q .snd)))
          (λ _ p → p)
          (λ v p → tr generating-fam-is-simplicial
                     (sym (□-retract-last₂ Ψ τ v)) p))
        □-comparable₂-is-inner-anodyne
}
}

\subtree[ub-002I]{
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Why the retraction has to fix the pair}

\p{Every convex #{A} is a retract of its cube, for instance by
#{r(x)_a = \bigvee_{b \preceq a} x_b}. But to transport the free case
(\ref{ub-002G}) from the cube to #{A} along a retraction, as \ref{ub-002H}
does, the retraction has to carry the comparability of #{x_i} and #{x_j} back
and forth, and it does so when it leaves both coordinates alone. The obvious
retraction fixes only the #{\preceq}-minimal coordinates, which is too few.
[The clamp](ub-002F) fixes whichever two coordinates are processed last, and
the price is that the processing order becomes data: the triangular form of
[a presentation](ub-002D). The reindexing of \ref{ub-002J} to \ref{ub-002M}
pays that price back, moving an arbitrary unrelated pair into the last two
places without permuting the cube.}
}

\subtree[ub-002J]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Coordinates, and the column of a presentation}

\p{A [presentation](ub-002D) lists a \em{row} per coordinate: how that
coordinate is constrained relative to the ones before it. The reindexing of
\ref{ub-002I} needs the \em{column}: for
a fixed coordinate #{i}, how every coordinate is constrained relative to
#{i}. Rows below #{i} are read off the column of #{i} directly; rows above it
are read off backwards, so a slot has to be reversed.}

\p{Coordinates are indexed with #{\Fin}, counting from the \em{last} — the
one processed last is \code{fzero} — since that is the direction the tower
recurses in.}

\agda{
rev : Slot → Slot
rev lo = hi
rev eq = eq
rev fr = fr
rev hi = lo

lab-at : ∀ {n} → Lab n → Fin n → Slot
lab-at {suc n} (ℓ , s) fzero = s
lab-at {suc n} (ℓ , s) (fsuc a) = lab-at ℓ a

□-coord : ∀ {n} → □^ n → Fin n → Δ¹
□-coord {suc zero} v fzero = v
□-coord {suc zero} v (fsuc ())
□-coord {suc (suc n)} (w , x) fzero = x
□-coord {suc (suc n)} (w , x) (fsuc a) = □-coord w a

col : ∀ {n} → Cvx n → Fin n → Lab n
col {suc n} (Ψ , ρ) fzero = (ρ , eq)
col {suc n} (Ψ , ρ) (fsuc i) = (col Ψ i , rev (lab-at ρ i))
}

\p{A row of constraints, read one coordinate at a time.}

\agda{
Bound-at
  : ∀ {n} (ℓ : Lab n) (w : □^ n) (t : Δ¹) → Bound ℓ w t
  → (a : Fin n) → bound¹ (lab-at ℓ a) (□-coord w a) t
Bound-at {suc zero} (_ , s) x t b fzero = b
Bound-at {suc zero} (_ , s) x t b (fsuc ())
Bound-at {suc (suc n)} (ℓ , s) (w , y) t (bs , b) fzero = b
Bound-at {suc (suc n)} (ℓ , s) (w , y) t (bs , b) (fsuc a)
  = Bound-at ℓ w t bs a

bound¹-rev : (c : Slot) (x y : Δ¹) → bound¹ c y x → bound¹ (rev c) x y
bound¹-rev lo x y p = p
bound¹-rev eq x y p = sym p
bound¹-rev fr x y p = ttᴸ
bound¹-rev hi x y p = p
}
}

\subtree[ub-002K]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Closed presentations}

\p{[Coherence](ub-002D) asks only that each row be monotone for the rows
below it, which is what [the retraction](ub-002F) needs and no more. The
reindexing needs the stronger condition that the presentation records the
constraints it \em{implies}, not only those it declares: a presentation is
\em{closed} when every column is monotone.}

\p{In the intended reading — the presentation is a preorder on the
coordinates — this is transitivity, and coherence is the special case of it
that ignores the coordinates above. So closure implies coherence, and the
proof is two projections.}

\agda{
is-closed : ∀ {n} → Cvx n → Type
is-closed {n} Ψ = (i : Fin n) → Mono Ψ (col Ψ i)

is-coherent←is-closed : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) → is-closed Ψ → is-coherent Ψ
is-coherent←is-closed {zero} _ _ = _
is-coherent←is-closed {suc n} (Ψ , ρ) cl
  = ( is-coherent←is-closed Ψ (λ i → cl (fsuc i) .fst)
    , cl fzero .fst )
}

\p{A column is the row of a coordinate, read in both directions, so on the
locus it is satisfied at that coordinate's own value.}

\agda{
Bound-col
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (i : Fin n) (w : □^ n)
  → Locus Ψ w → Bound (col Ψ i) w (□-coord w i)
Bound-col {suc zero} (Ψ , ρ) fzero x l = refl
Bound-col {suc zero} (Ψ , ρ) (fsuc ()) x l
Bound-col {suc (suc n)} (Ψ , ρ) fzero (w , x) (lw , b) = (b , refl)
Bound-col {suc (suc n)} (Ψ , ρ) (fsuc i) (w , x) (lw , b)
  = ( Bound-col Ψ i w lw
    , bound¹-rev (lab-at ρ i) x (□-coord w i) (Bound-at ρ w x b i) )
}
}

\subtree[ub-002L]{
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Two columns of an unrelated pair}

\p{The one thing the reindexing needs beyond [the columns](ub-002J): if #{i}
and #{j} are unrelated, then adding a copy of #{x_j} on top of a presentation
that already carries a copy of #{x_i} is again coherent. In slots that reads
as a compatibility between the two columns, and it comes out of the rows the
presentation already has — closure is not needed for it, only
[monotonicity](ub-002D) of the row being extended.}

\p{Two scalar facts do the work. The first is a symmetry: over an unrelated
pair the compatibility condition does not care which of the two slots is
which. The second reverses a slot, which is what happens when a column is
read against a row above it.}

\agda{
compat¹-fr-sym : (a b : Slot) → compat¹ a b fr → compat¹ b a fr
compat¹-fr-sym lo lo p = tt
compat¹-fr-sym lo eq p = ¡ p
compat¹-fr-sym lo fr p = tt
compat¹-fr-sym lo hi p = ¡ p
compat¹-fr-sym eq lo p = ¡ (p .snd)
compat¹-fr-sym eq eq p = ¡ (p .fst)
compat¹-fr-sym eq fr p = tt
compat¹-fr-sym eq hi p = ¡ (p .fst)
compat¹-fr-sym fr lo p = tt
compat¹-fr-sym fr eq p = (tt , tt)
compat¹-fr-sym fr fr p = tt
compat¹-fr-sym fr hi p = tt
compat¹-fr-sym hi lo p = ¡ p
compat¹-fr-sym hi eq p = ¡ p
compat¹-fr-sym hi fr p = tt
compat¹-fr-sym hi hi p = tt

compat¹-rev : (c s : Slot) → compat¹ c fr s → compat¹ s (rev c) fr
compat¹-rev lo lo p = ¡ p
compat¹-rev lo eq p = ¡ p
compat¹-rev lo fr p = tt
compat¹-rev lo hi p = tt
compat¹-rev eq lo p = ¡ (p .fst)
compat¹-rev eq eq p = ¡ (p .fst)
compat¹-rev eq fr p = tt
compat¹-rev eq hi p = ¡ (p .snd)
compat¹-rev fr lo p = tt
compat¹-rev fr eq p = (tt , tt)
compat¹-rev fr fr p = tt
compat¹-rev fr hi p = tt
compat¹-rev hi lo p = tt
compat¹-rev hi eq p = ¡ p
compat¹-rev hi fr p = tt
compat¹-rev hi hi p = ¡ p
}

\agda{
Compat-at
  : ∀ {n} (ℓ τ : Lab n) (s : Slot) → Compat ℓ τ s
  → (a : Fin n) → compat¹ (lab-at ℓ a) (lab-at τ a) s
Compat-at {suc n} (ℓ , a) (τ , b) s (cs , c) fzero = c
Compat-at {suc n} (ℓ , a) (τ , b) s (cs , c) (fsuc i) = Compat-at ℓ τ s cs i

Compat-fr-sym
  : ∀ {n} (ℓ τ : Lab n) → Compat ℓ τ fr → Compat τ ℓ fr
Compat-fr-sym {zero} _ _ _ = tt
Compat-fr-sym {suc n} (ℓ , a) (τ , b) (cs , c)
  = (Compat-fr-sym ℓ τ cs , compat¹-fr-sym a b c)

Compat-col
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (ρ : Lab n) → Mono Ψ ρ
  → (j : Fin n) → lab-at ρ j ＝ fr → Compat ρ (col Ψ j) fr
Compat-col {suc n} (Ψ , ℓ) (ρ , s) (mρ , cm) fzero h
  = ( Compat-fr-sym ℓ ρ (tr (Compat ℓ ρ) h cm)
    , tr (λ z → compat¹ z eq fr) (sym h) tt )
Compat-col {suc n} (Ψ , ℓ) (ρ , s) (mρ , cm) (fsuc j) h
  = ( Compat-col Ψ ρ mρ j h
    , compat¹-rev (lab-at ℓ j) s
        (tr (λ z → compat¹ (lab-at ℓ j) z s) h (Compat-at ℓ ρ s cm j)) )
}

\p{A coordinate's column is #{=} at that coordinate, which is what pins the
copy to the original.}

\agda{
col-diag : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (i : Fin n) → lab-at (col Ψ i) i ＝ eq
col-diag {suc n} (Ψ , ρ) fzero = refl
col-diag {suc n} (Ψ , ρ) (fsuc i) = col-diag Ψ i

col-coord
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (i : Fin n) (w : □^ n) (s : Δ¹)
  → Bound (col Ψ i) w s → □-coord w i ＝ s
col-coord Ψ i w s b
  = tr (λ z → bound¹ z (□-coord w i) s) (col-diag Ψ i)
      (Bound-at (col Ψ i) w s b i)
}
}

\subtree[ub-002M]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Comparability of any pair over a convex subcube}

\p{Step 1 of [the outline](Synthetic.Categories.ConvexSubcubes), for an
arbitrary pair. Let #{\Psi} be a [closed](ub-002K) convex presentation of
#{\square^{n}} with locus #{A}, and let #{i, j} be two coordinates that
#{\Psi} leaves unrelated. Then}

##{A \wedge \square^{n}_{(i,j)} \hookrightarrow A}

\p{is inner anodyne.}

\p{The reduction to [the last-two case](ub-002H) needs no permutation of the
cube. Stack two more coordinates on #{\Psi}: one pinned to #{x_i} and one
pinned to #{x_j}, each by [its column](ub-002J), whose diagonal slot is #{=}.
The two rows are monotone because #{\Psi} is closed, and they leave the two
new coordinates unrelated because #{i} and #{j} are unrelated
(\ref{ub-002L}). Writing #{A^+ \subseteq \square^{n+2}} for the locus of the
extended presentation, forgetting the two new coordinates is an equivalence
#{A^+ \simeq A}, since the #{=} slots determine what was added, and under it
the comparability of the last two coordinates is the comparability of #{x_i}
and #{x_j}. So the two inclusions are equivalent in the arrow category,}

\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}
	{A^+ \wedge \square^{n+2}_{(n,n+1)}} && {A \wedge \square^{n}_{(i,j)}} \\
	\\
	{A^+} && A
	\arrow["\simeq", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[hook, from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[hook, from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["\simeq"', from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}
}

\p{and the left-hand one is inner anodyne by \ref{ub-002H}.}

\agda{
comparable-pair
  : ∀ {m} (Ψ : Cvx (suc m)) (i j : Fin (suc m))
  → Σ (□^ (suc m)) (Locus Ψ) → Type 𝓘
comparable-pair Ψ i j q
  = generating-fam-is-simplicial (□-coord (q .fst) i , □-coord (q .fst) j)

module _
  {m : ℕ} (Ψ : Cvx (suc m)) (cl : is-closed Ψ)
  (i j : Fin (suc m)) (ij : lab-at (col Ψ i) j ＝ fr)
  where

  private
    Ψ⁺ : Cvx (suc (suc (suc m)))
    Ψ⁺ = cvx-ext (Ψ , col Ψ i) (col Ψ j)

    Top Bot : Type 𝓘
    Top = Σ (□^ (suc (suc (suc m)))) (Locus Ψ⁺)
    Bot = Σ (□^ (suc m)) (Locus Ψ)

    down : Top → Bot
    down (((w , s) , t) , ((lw , bi) , (bj , _))) = (w , lw)

    up : Bot → Top
    up (w , lw)
      = ( ((w , □-coord w i) , □-coord w j)
        , ( (lw , Bound-col Ψ i w lw)
          , (Bound-col Ψ j w lw , ttᴸ) ) )

    pin : (e : Top) → (□-coord (down e .fst) i , □-coord (down e .fst) j)
        ＝ □-last₂ (e .fst)
    pin (((w , s) , t) , ((lw , bi) , (bj , _)))
      = ×-path→ (col-coord Ψ i w s bi , col-coord Ψ j w t bj)

    up-down : (e : Top) → up (down e) ＝ e
    up-down e@(((w , s) , t) , ((lw , bi) , (bj , _)))
      = subtype-ext (Locus-is-prop Ψ⁺)
          (×-path→ ( ×-path→ (refl , col-coord Ψ i w s bi)
                   , col-coord Ψ j w t bj ))

    down-up : (b : Bot) → down (up b) ＝ b
    down-up b = refl

    bot≃ : Top ≃ Bot
    bot≃ = mk≃ down (is-equiv←qinv (up , up-down , down-up))

    top-down
      : Σ Top (comparable-on (Locus Ψ⁺)) → Σ Bot (comparable-pair Ψ i j)
    top-down (e , p) = (down e , tr generating-fam-is-simplicial (sym (pin e)) p)

    top-up
      : Σ Bot (comparable-pair Ψ i j) → Σ Top (comparable-on (Locus Ψ⁺))
    top-up (b , q) = (up b , q)

    top-η : (x : Σ Top (comparable-on (Locus Ψ⁺))) → top-up (top-down x) ＝ x
    top-η (e , p)
      = subtype-ext (λ e' → generating-fam-is-simplicial-is-prop (□-last₂ (e' .fst)))
          (up-down e)

    top-ε : (x : Σ Bot (comparable-pair Ψ i j)) → top-down (top-up x) ＝ x
    top-ε (b , q)
      = subtype-ext (λ b' → generating-fam-is-simplicial-is-prop _) refl

    top≃ : Σ Top (comparable-on (Locus Ψ⁺)) ≃ Σ Bot (comparable-pair Ψ i j)
    top≃ = mk≃ top-down (is-equiv←qinv (top-up , top-η , top-ε))

  convex-pair-comparability-is-inner-anodyne
    : is-inner-anodyne (fst {B = comparable-pair Ψ i j})
  convex-pair-comparability-is-inner-anodyne
    = is-anodyne←equiv fst fst
        (mk-arr-equiv top≃ bot≃ (λ _ → refl))
        Λ[2,1]-incl
        (convex-comparability-is-inner-anodyne
           (Ψ , col Ψ i) (col Ψ j)
           (is-coherent←is-closed Ψ cl , cl i)
           (cl j , Compat-col Ψ (col Ψ i) (cl i) j ij))
}
}

\subtree[ub-002N]{
\taxon{Example}
\title{An instance in dimension three}

\p{So that the theorem is visibly not vacuous, and to fix the conventions:
coordinates are indexed from the last, so on
#{\square^3 = (\Delta^1\times\Delta^1)\times\Delta^1} the index \code{fzero}
is #{x_2}, and the presentation below reads}

##{x_0 \le x_1, \qquad x_0 \le x_2, \qquad x_1 \parallel x_2.}

\p{It is closed — the only relations its two generators imply are themselves —
so [the theorem](ub-002M) applies at the pair #{(x_2, x_1)}. Writing #{A} for
the locus, that is}

##{A \wedge \square^3_{(1,2)} \hookrightarrow A,}

\p{and it is inner anodyne. Neither side is a product of simplices and neither
map is a projection, so this is not an instance of anything above it.}

\agda{
private
  ex : Cvx 3
  ex = (((tt , tt) , (tt , lo)) , ((tt , lo) , fr))

  ex-is-closed : is-closed ex
  ex-is-closed fzero = _
  ex-is-closed (fsuc fzero) = _
  ex-is-closed (fsuc (fsuc fzero)) = _

  ex-unrelated : lab-at (col ex fzero) (fsuc fzero) ＝ fr
  ex-unrelated = refl

ex-comparability-is-inner-anodyne
  : is-inner-anodyne
      (fst {B = comparable-pair ex fzero (fsuc fzero)})
ex-comparability-is-inner-anodyne
  = convex-pair-comparability-is-inner-anodyne
      ex ex-is-closed fzero (fsuc fzero) ex-unrelated
}
}

\subtree[ub-002P]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Joining slots, and the empty presentation}

\p{Two presentations of the same cube are conjoined slot by slot, in the
order that reads a slot as \em{how much is being asked}:
#{\parallel} asks nothing, #{\le} and #{\ge} ask one inequality, #{=} asks
both. That order is a join semilattice, and a bound in a joined slot is
exactly the two bounds together. It is not the order #{\sqsubseteq} of
\ref{ub-002C}, which is about the clamp rather than about constraints.}

\agda{
_⋎_ : Slot → Slot → Slot
lo ⋎ lo = lo
lo ⋎ eq = eq
lo ⋎ fr = lo
lo ⋎ hi = eq
eq ⋎ _ = eq
fr ⋎ s = s
hi ⋎ lo = eq
hi ⋎ eq = eq
hi ⋎ fr = hi
hi ⋎ hi = hi

bound¹-⋎←
  : (s t : Slot) {x y : Δ¹}
  → bound¹ s x y → bound¹ t x y → bound¹ (s ⋎ t) x y
bound¹-⋎← lo lo p q = p
bound¹-⋎← lo eq p q = q
bound¹-⋎← lo fr p q = p
bound¹-⋎← lo hi p q = ≤-antisym p q
bound¹-⋎← eq _ p q = p
bound¹-⋎← fr _ p q = q
bound¹-⋎← hi lo p q = ≤-antisym q p
bound¹-⋎← hi eq p q = q
bound¹-⋎← hi fr p q = p
bound¹-⋎← hi hi p q = p

bound¹-⋎→₁
  : (s t : Slot) {x y : Δ¹} → bound¹ (s ⋎ t) x y → bound¹ s x y
bound¹-⋎→₁ lo lo p = p
bound¹-⋎→₁ lo eq p = ≤←＝ p
bound¹-⋎→₁ lo fr p = p
bound¹-⋎→₁ lo hi p = ≤←＝ p
bound¹-⋎→₁ eq _ p = p
bound¹-⋎→₁ fr _ p = ttᴸ
bound¹-⋎→₁ hi lo p = ≥←＝ p
bound¹-⋎→₁ hi eq p = ≥←＝ p
bound¹-⋎→₁ hi fr p = p
bound¹-⋎→₁ hi hi p = p

bound¹-⋎→₂
  : (s t : Slot) {x y : Δ¹} → bound¹ (s ⋎ t) x y → bound¹ t x y
bound¹-⋎→₂ lo lo p = p
bound¹-⋎→₂ lo eq p = p
bound¹-⋎→₂ lo fr p = ttᴸ
bound¹-⋎→₂ lo hi p = ≥←＝ p
bound¹-⋎→₂ eq lo p = ≤←＝ p
bound¹-⋎→₂ eq eq p = p
bound¹-⋎→₂ eq fr p = ttᴸ
bound¹-⋎→₂ eq hi p = ≥←＝ p
bound¹-⋎→₂ fr _ p = p
bound¹-⋎→₂ hi lo p = ≤←＝ p
bound¹-⋎→₂ hi eq p = p
bound¹-⋎→₂ hi fr p = ttᴸ
bound¹-⋎→₂ hi hi p = p
}

\p{The join is commutative, and reversing a slot distributes over it. Both are
case analyses, and they are what makes the column of an extended presentation
come out symmetric in \ref{ub-002Q}.}

\agda{
⋎-comm : (s t : Slot) → s ⋎ t ＝ t ⋎ s
⋎-comm lo lo = refl
⋎-comm lo eq = refl
⋎-comm lo fr = refl
⋎-comm lo hi = refl
⋎-comm eq lo = refl
⋎-comm eq eq = refl
⋎-comm eq fr = refl
⋎-comm eq hi = refl
⋎-comm fr lo = refl
⋎-comm fr eq = refl
⋎-comm fr fr = refl
⋎-comm fr hi = refl
⋎-comm hi lo = refl
⋎-comm hi eq = refl
⋎-comm hi fr = refl
⋎-comm hi hi = refl

rev-⋎ : (s t : Slot) → rev (s ⋎ t) ＝ rev s ⋎ rev t
rev-⋎ lo lo = refl
rev-⋎ lo eq = refl
rev-⋎ lo fr = refl
rev-⋎ lo hi = refl
rev-⋎ eq lo = refl
rev-⋎ eq eq = refl
rev-⋎ eq fr = refl
rev-⋎ eq hi = refl
rev-⋎ fr lo = refl
rev-⋎ fr eq = refl
rev-⋎ fr fr = refl
rev-⋎ fr hi = refl
rev-⋎ hi lo = refl
rev-⋎ hi eq = refl
rev-⋎ hi fr = refl
rev-⋎ hi hi = refl

rev-rev : (s : Slot) → rev (rev s) ＝ s
rev-rev lo = refl
rev-rev eq = refl
rev-rev fr = refl
rev-rev hi = refl
}

\p{The empty presentation constrains nothing. It is closed, its locus is the
whole cube, and it is where a list of relations is adjoined from.}

\agda{
fr-lab : ∀ {n} → Lab n
fr-lab {zero} = tt
fr-lab {suc n} = (fr-lab , fr)

discrete : ∀ {n} → Cvx n
discrete {zero} = tt
discrete {suc n} = (discrete , fr-lab)

Bound-fr : ∀ {n} (v : □^ n) (t : Δ¹) → Bound (fr-lab {n}) v t
Bound-fr {zero} v t = ttᴸ
Bound-fr {suc zero} v t = ttᴸ
Bound-fr {suc (suc n)} (w , x) t = (Bound-fr w t , ttᴸ)

Locus-discrete : ∀ {n} (v : □^ n) → Locus (discrete {n}) v
Locus-discrete {zero} v = ttᴸ
Locus-discrete {suc zero} v = ttᴸ
Locus-discrete {suc (suc n)} (w , t) = (Locus-discrete w , Bound-fr w t)

Compat-fr : ∀ {n} (τ : Lab n) (s : Slot) → Compat (fr-lab {n}) τ s
Compat-fr {zero} τ s = tt
Compat-fr {suc n} (τ , b) s = (Compat-fr τ s , tt)

Mono-discrete : ∀ {n} (ρ : Lab n) → Mono (discrete {n}) ρ
Mono-discrete {zero} ρ = tt
Mono-discrete {suc n} (τ , s) = (Mono-discrete τ , Compat-fr τ s)

discrete-is-closed : ∀ {n} → is-closed (discrete {n})
discrete-is-closed i = Mono-discrete (col discrete i)
}
}

\subtree[ub-002Q]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Adjoining one relation}

\p{The meet of two [closed](ub-002K) presentations has to be closed again,
and it is computed one relation at a time. Adjoining a \em{single} relation
#{x_a \le x_b} to a presentation that is already closed has a one-step
formula — the classical one,}

##{x \preceq' y \iff x \preceq y \ \lor\ (x \preceq a \ \land\ b \preceq y),}

\p{with no iteration and nothing to terminate. A meet is then a fold: adjoin
the second presentation's relations to the first one at a time.}

\p{In slots, #{x \preceq a} is #{\le} or #{=} in [the column of #{a}](ub-002J)
at #{x}, and #{b \preceq y} is #{\ge} or #{=} in the column of #{b} at #{y}.
The two conjunctions are one operation.}

\agda{
push : Slot → Slot → Slot
push lo hi = lo
push lo eq = lo
push eq hi = lo
push eq eq = lo
push _ _ = fr

push-sound
  : (p q : Slot) {x α β y : Δ¹}
  → bound¹ p x α → bound¹ q y β → α ≤ β → bound¹ (push p q) x y
push-sound lo eq bp bq h = ≤-trans bp (≤-trans h (≥←＝ bq))
push-sound lo hi bp bq h = ≤-trans bp (≤-trans h bq)
push-sound eq eq bp bq h = ≤-trans (≤←＝ bp) (≤-trans h (≥←＝ bq))
push-sound eq hi bp bq h = ≤-trans (≤←＝ bp) (≤-trans h bq)
push-sound lo lo _ _ _ = ttᴸ
push-sound lo fr _ _ _ = ttᴸ
push-sound eq lo _ _ _ = ttᴸ
push-sound eq fr _ _ _ = ttᴸ
push-sound fr _ _ _ _ = ttᴸ
push-sound hi _ _ _ _ = ttᴸ
}

\p{One row of the extended presentation, then the whole of it. The two extra
slots are the column entries at the row's own coordinate.}

\agda{
addL : ∀ {n} → Lab n → Lab n → Lab n → Slot → Slot → Lab n
addL {zero} ρ LA UB la⋆ ub⋆ = tt
addL {suc n} (ρ , s) (LA , la) (UB , ub) la⋆ ub⋆
  = ( addL ρ LA UB la⋆ ub⋆
    , s ⋎ (push la ub⋆ ⋎ rev (push la⋆ ub)) )

addC : ∀ {n} → Cvx n → Lab n → Lab n → Cvx n
addC {zero} Ψ LA UB = tt
addC {suc n} (Ψ , ρ) (LA , la) (UB , ub) = (addC Ψ LA UB , addL ρ LA UB la ub)

add : ∀ {n} → Cvx n → Fin n → Fin n → Cvx n
add Ψ a b = addC Ψ (col Ψ a) (col Ψ b)
}
}

\subtree[ub-002R]{
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The locus of an extension}

\p{Adjoining #{x_a \le x_b} cuts out exactly that: the locus of the extension
is the locus of the original meet the half-space. Both directions are wanted,
since it is the two of them together that make a list of relations a
presentation of the subtype it names.}

\p{One direction is [the one-step formula](ub-002Q) read as an implication —
every slot it adds is implied by #{x_a \le x_b} and the original bounds,
through the reading of the columns on the locus (\ref{ub-002K}).}

\agda{
addL-sound
  : ∀ {n} (ρ LA UB : Lab n) (la⋆ ub⋆ : Slot) (v : □^ n) (t α β : Δ¹)
  → Bound ρ v t → Bound LA v α → Bound UB v β
  → bound¹ la⋆ t α → bound¹ ub⋆ t β → α ≤ β
  → Bound (addL ρ LA UB la⋆ ub⋆) v t
addL-sound {zero} ρ LA UB la⋆ ub⋆ v t α β br bl bu pl pu h
  = ttᴸ
addL-sound {suc zero} (_ , s) (_ , la) (_ , ub) la⋆ ub⋆ x t α β br bl bu pl pu h
  = bound¹-⋎← s (push la ub⋆ ⋎ rev (push la⋆ ub)) br
      (bound¹-⋎← (push la ub⋆) (rev (push la⋆ ub))
        (push-sound la ub⋆ bl pu h)
        (bound¹-rev (push la⋆ ub) x t (push-sound la⋆ ub pl bu h)))
addL-sound {suc (suc n)} (ρ , s) (LA , la) (UB , ub) la⋆ ub⋆
           (w , x) t α β (br , bs) (bl , bx) (bu , bux) pl pu h
  = ( addL-sound ρ LA UB la⋆ ub⋆ w t α β br bl bu pl pu h
    , bound¹-⋎← s (push la ub⋆ ⋎ rev (push la⋆ ub)) bs
        (bound¹-⋎← (push la ub⋆) (rev (push la⋆ ub))
          (push-sound la ub⋆ bx pu h)
          (bound¹-rev (push la⋆ ub) x t (push-sound la⋆ ub pl bux h))) )

addC-sound
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (LA UB : Lab n) (v : □^ n) (α β : Δ¹)
  → Locus Ψ v → Bound LA v α → Bound UB v β → α ≤ β
  → Locus (addC Ψ LA UB) v
addC-sound {zero} Ψ LA UB v α β l bl bu h = ttᴸ
addC-sound {suc zero} Ψ LA UB v α β l bl bu h = ttᴸ
addC-sound {suc (suc n)} (Ψ , ρ) (LA , la) (UB , ub) (w , t) α β
           (l , br) (bl , pl) (bu , pu) h
  = ( addC-sound Ψ LA UB w α β l bl bu h
    , addL-sound ρ LA UB la ub w t α β br bl bu pl pu h )

add-sound
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (a b : Fin n) (v : □^ n)
  → Locus Ψ v → □-coord v a ≤ □-coord v b → Locus (add Ψ a b) v
add-sound Ψ a b v l h
  = addC-sound Ψ (col Ψ a) (col Ψ b) v (□-coord v a) (□-coord v b) l
      (Bound-col Ψ a v l) (Bound-col Ψ b v l) h
}

\p{The other direction has two halves. Forgetting the adjoined slots is a
projection, one \code{⋎} at a time.}

\agda{
addL-forget
  : ∀ {n} (ρ LA UB : Lab n) (la⋆ ub⋆ : Slot) (v : □^ n) (t : Δ¹)
  → Bound (addL ρ LA UB la⋆ ub⋆) v t → Bound ρ v t
addL-forget {zero} ρ LA UB la⋆ ub⋆ v t b = ttᴸ
addL-forget {suc zero} (_ , s) (_ , la) (_ , ub) la⋆ ub⋆ x t b
  = bound¹-⋎→₁ s (push la ub⋆ ⋎ rev (push la⋆ ub)) b
addL-forget {suc (suc n)} (ρ , s) (LA , la) (UB , ub) la⋆ ub⋆ (w , x) t (br , bs)
  = ( addL-forget ρ LA UB la⋆ ub⋆ w t br
    , bound¹-⋎→₁ s (push la ub⋆ ⋎ rev (push la⋆ ub)) bs )

addC-forget
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (LA UB : Lab n) (v : □^ n)
  → Locus (addC Ψ LA UB) v → Locus Ψ v
addC-forget {zero} Ψ LA UB v l = ttᴸ
addC-forget {suc zero} Ψ LA UB v l = ttᴸ
addC-forget {suc (suc n)} (Ψ , ρ) (LA , la) (UB , ub) (w , t) (l , br)
  = ( addC-forget Ψ LA UB w l
    , addL-forget ρ LA UB la ub w t br )
}

\p{The other half is that the adjoined relation is really recorded. Reading
a column off an extension is the same operation as building a row of one,
which is where [the join's commutativity](ub-002P) is spent; at the pair
#{(a,b)} the two diagonal #{=} slots then leave a #{\ge} behind, and that is
the constraint.}

\agda{
lab-at-addL
  : ∀ {n} (ρ LA UB : Lab n) (la⋆ ub⋆ : Slot) (i : Fin n)
  → lab-at (addL ρ LA UB la⋆ ub⋆) i
    ＝ lab-at ρ i ⋎ (push (lab-at LA i) ub⋆ ⋎ rev (push la⋆ (lab-at UB i)))
lab-at-addL {suc n} (ρ , s) (LA , la) (UB , ub) la⋆ ub⋆ fzero = refl
lab-at-addL {suc n} (ρ , s) (LA , la) (UB , ub) la⋆ ub⋆ (fsuc i)
  = lab-at-addL ρ LA UB la⋆ ub⋆ i

col-addC
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (LA UB : Lab n) (i : Fin n)
  → col (addC Ψ LA UB) i ＝ addL (col Ψ i) LA UB (lab-at LA i) (lab-at UB i)
col-addC {suc n} (Ψ , ρ) (LA , la) (UB , ub) fzero = refl
col-addC {suc n} (Ψ , ρ) (LA , la) (UB , ub) (fsuc i)
  = ×-path→
      ( col-addC Ψ LA UB i
      , ap rev (lab-at-addL ρ LA UB la ub i)
        ∙ rev-⋎ (lab-at ρ i)
            (push (lab-at LA i) ub ⋎ rev (push la (lab-at UB i)))
        ∙ ap (rev (lab-at ρ i) ⋎_)
            ( rev-⋎ (push (lab-at LA i) ub) (rev (push la (lab-at UB i)))
            ∙ ap (rev (push (lab-at LA i) ub) ⋎_)
                (rev-rev (push la (lab-at UB i)))
            ∙ ⋎-comm (rev (push (lab-at LA i) ub)) (push la (lab-at UB i)) ) )
}

\agda{
module _ {n : ℕ} (Ψ : Cvx n) (a b : Fin n) where

  private
    P Q : Slot
    P = lab-at (col Ψ a) b
    Q = lab-at (col Ψ b) a

    slot-path : lab-at (col (add Ψ a b) a) b ＝ P ⋎ (push P Q ⋎ hi)
    slot-path
      = ap (λ z → lab-at z b) (col-addC Ψ (col Ψ a) (col Ψ b) a)
      ∙ lab-at-addL (col Ψ a) (col Ψ a) (col Ψ b)
          (lab-at (col Ψ a) a) (lab-at (col Ψ b) a) b
      ∙ ap (λ s → P ⋎ (push P Q ⋎ rev s))
          ( ap (λ z → push z (lab-at (col Ψ b) b)) (col-diag Ψ a)
          ∙ ap (push eq) (col-diag Ψ b) )

  add-≤
    : (v : □^ n) → Locus (add Ψ a b) v → □-coord v a ≤ □-coord v b
  add-≤ v l
    = bound¹-⋎→₂ (push P Q) hi
        (bound¹-⋎→₂ P (push P Q ⋎ hi)
          (tr (λ s → bound¹ s (□-coord v b) (□-coord v a)) slot-path
            (Bound-at (col (add Ψ a b) a) v (□-coord v a)
              (Bound-col (add Ψ a b) a v l) b)))
}
}

\subtree[ub-002S]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Reading a slot as two relations}

\p{A slot carries two independent facts, #{x \preceq y} and #{y \preceq x},
and every operation on slots above is a pair of operations on those. Naming
them turns the case analyses into ordinary relational reasoning, which is what
[the closedness of an extension](ub-002T) needs: there the argument is about
paths, and a case bash in nine slot variables is not the way to do it.}

\agda{
le ge : Slot → Type
le lo = 𝟙
le eq = 𝟙
le fr = ∅
le hi = ∅
ge lo = ∅
ge eq = 𝟙
ge fr = ∅
ge hi = 𝟙
}

\p{The slot order is the two facts read contravariantly, and the
[compatibility condition](ub-002D) is the slot order read twice.}

\agda{
⊑-mk : (s t : Slot) → (ge s → ge t) → (le t → le s) → s ⊑ t
⊑-mk lo lo f g = tt
⊑-mk lo eq f g = tt
⊑-mk lo fr f g = tt
⊑-mk lo hi f g = tt
⊑-mk eq lo f g = f tt
⊑-mk eq eq f g = tt
⊑-mk eq fr f g = f tt
⊑-mk eq hi f g = tt
⊑-mk fr lo f g = g tt
⊑-mk fr eq f g = g tt
⊑-mk fr fr f g = tt
⊑-mk fr hi f g = tt
⊑-mk hi lo f g = f tt
⊑-mk hi eq f g = g tt
⊑-mk hi fr f g = f tt
⊑-mk hi hi f g = tt

⊑-ge : (s t : Slot) → s ⊑ t → ge s → ge t
⊑-ge lo t p q = ¡ q
⊑-ge eq eq p q = tt
⊑-ge eq hi p q = tt
⊑-ge fr t p q = ¡ q
⊑-ge hi hi p q = tt

⊑-le : (s t : Slot) → s ⊑ t → le t → le s
⊑-le lo t p q = tt
⊑-le eq eq p q = tt
⊑-le eq hi p q = ¡ q
⊑-le fr fr p q = ¡ q
⊑-le fr hi p q = ¡ q
⊑-le hi hi p q = ¡ q

compat¹-mk
  : (a b s : Slot) → (le a → b ⊑ s) → (ge a → s ⊑ b) → compat¹ a b s
compat¹-mk lo b s f g = f tt
compat¹-mk eq b s f g = (f tt , g tt)
compat¹-mk fr b s f g = tt
compat¹-mk hi b s f g = g tt

compat¹-le : (a b s : Slot) → compat¹ a b s → le a → b ⊑ s
compat¹-le lo b s p q = p
compat¹-le eq b s p q = p .fst
compat¹-le fr b s p q = ¡ q
compat¹-le hi b s p q = ¡ q

compat¹-ge : (a b s : Slot) → compat¹ a b s → ge a → s ⊑ b
compat¹-ge lo b s p q = ¡ q
compat¹-ge eq b s p q = p .snd
compat¹-ge fr b s p q = ¡ q
compat¹-ge hi b s p q = p
}

\p{The join is a disjunction on each side; \code{push} is a conjunction on the
#{\preceq} side and empty on the other; reversal swaps them.}

\agda{
le-⋎ : (s t : Slot) → le (s ⋎ t) → le s ⊎ le t
le-⋎ lo lo p = inl tt
le-⋎ lo eq p = inl tt
le-⋎ lo fr p = inl tt
le-⋎ lo hi p = inl tt
le-⋎ eq t p = inl tt
le-⋎ fr t p = inr p
le-⋎ hi lo p = inr tt
le-⋎ hi eq p = inr tt
le-⋎ hi fr p = ¡ p
le-⋎ hi hi p = ¡ p

le-⋎₁ : (s t : Slot) → le s → le (s ⋎ t)
le-⋎₁ lo lo p = tt
le-⋎₁ lo eq p = tt
le-⋎₁ lo fr p = tt
le-⋎₁ lo hi p = tt
le-⋎₁ eq t p = tt
le-⋎₁ fr t p = ¡ p
le-⋎₁ hi t p = ¡ p

le-⋎₂ : (s t : Slot) → le t → le (s ⋎ t)
le-⋎₂ lo lo p = tt
le-⋎₂ lo eq p = tt
le-⋎₂ lo fr p = ¡ p
le-⋎₂ lo hi p = ¡ p
le-⋎₂ eq t p = tt
le-⋎₂ fr t p = p
le-⋎₂ hi lo p = tt
le-⋎₂ hi eq p = tt
le-⋎₂ hi fr p = ¡ p
le-⋎₂ hi hi p = ¡ p

le-rev : (s : Slot) → le (rev s) → ge s
le-rev lo p = ¡ p
le-rev eq p = tt
le-rev fr p = p
le-rev hi p = tt

le-rev← : (s : Slot) → ge s → le (rev s)
le-rev← lo p = ¡ p
le-rev← eq p = tt
le-rev← fr p = p
le-rev← hi p = tt

le-push : (p q : Slot) → le (push p q) → le p × ge q
le-push lo hi r = (tt , tt)
le-push lo eq r = (tt , tt)
le-push eq hi r = (tt , tt)
le-push eq eq r = (tt , tt)
le-push lo lo r = ¡ r
le-push lo fr r = ¡ r
le-push eq lo r = ¡ r
le-push eq fr r = ¡ r
le-push fr q r = ¡ r
le-push hi q r = ¡ r

le-push← : (p q : Slot) → le p → ge q → le (push p q)
le-push← lo lo u w = ¡ w
le-push← lo eq u w = tt
le-push← lo fr u w = ¡ w
le-push← lo hi u w = tt
le-push← eq lo u w = ¡ w
le-push← eq eq u w = tt
le-push← eq fr u w = ¡ w
le-push← eq hi u w = tt
le-push← fr q u w = ¡ u
le-push← hi q u w = ¡ u

ge-rev : (s : Slot) → ge (rev s) → le s
ge-rev lo p = tt
ge-rev eq p = tt
ge-rev fr p = p
ge-rev hi p = p

ge-rev← : (s : Slot) → le s → ge (rev s)
ge-rev← lo p = tt
ge-rev← eq p = tt
ge-rev← fr p = p
ge-rev← hi p = p

ge-push : (p q : Slot) → ge (push p q) → ∅
ge-push lo lo r = r
ge-push lo eq r = r
ge-push lo fr r = r
ge-push lo hi r = r
ge-push eq lo r = r
ge-push eq eq r = r
ge-push eq fr r = r
ge-push eq hi r = r
ge-push fr q r = r
ge-push hi q r = r
}
}

\subtree[ub-002T]{
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Closedness, read at a triple}

\p{[Closedness](ub-002K) is stated level by level, which is what
[the retraction](ub-002F) wants but not what a transitivity argument wants.
It is equivalent to the pointwise condition at every triple of coordinates,
and the translation is an induction with no case analysis on which coordinate
is deeper than which — the four cases of a pair of #{\Fin}s already sort that
out.}

\agda{
rel : ∀ {n} → Cvx n → Fin n → Fin n → Slot
rel Ψ x y = lab-at (col Ψ y) x

rel-rev : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (x y : Fin n) → rel Ψ x y ＝ rev (rel Ψ y x)
rel-rev {suc n} (Ψ , ρ) fzero fzero = refl
rel-rev {suc n} (Ψ , ρ) fzero (fsuc y) = refl
rel-rev {suc n} (Ψ , ρ) (fsuc x) fzero = sym (rev-rev (lab-at ρ x))
rel-rev {suc n} (Ψ , ρ) (fsuc x) (fsuc y) = rel-rev Ψ x y

⊑-refl : (s : Slot) → s ⊑ s
⊑-refl lo = tt
⊑-refl eq = tt
⊑-refl fr = tt
⊑-refl hi = tt

compat¹-swap : (a b s : Slot) → compat¹ a b s → compat¹ (rev a) s b
compat¹-swap lo b s p = p
compat¹-swap eq b s p = (p .snd , p .fst)
compat¹-swap fr b s p = tt
compat¹-swap hi b s p = p
}

\agda{
Compat-mk
  : ∀ {n} (ℓ τ : Lab n) (s : Slot)
  → ((x : Fin n) → compat¹ (lab-at ℓ x) (lab-at τ x) s) → Compat ℓ τ s
Compat-mk {zero} ℓ τ s h = tt
Compat-mk {suc n} (ℓ , a) (τ , b) s h
  = (Compat-mk ℓ τ s (λ x → h (fsuc x)) , h fzero)

Mono-at
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (ρ : Lab n) → Mono Ψ ρ
  → (x y : Fin n) → compat¹ (rel Ψ x y) (lab-at ρ x) (lab-at ρ y)
Mono-at {suc n} (Ψ , ρ₊) (ρ₀ , s) (m , c) fzero fzero
  = (⊑-refl s , ⊑-refl s)
Mono-at {suc n} (Ψ , ρ₊) (ρ₀ , s) (m , c) (fsuc x) fzero
  = Compat-at ρ₊ ρ₀ s c x
Mono-at {suc n} (Ψ , ρ₊) (ρ₀ , s) (m , c) fzero (fsuc y)
  = compat¹-swap (lab-at ρ₊ y) (lab-at ρ₀ y) s (Compat-at ρ₊ ρ₀ s c y)
Mono-at {suc n} (Ψ , ρ₊) (ρ₀ , s) (m , c) (fsuc x) (fsuc y)
  = Mono-at Ψ ρ₀ m x y

Mono-mk
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (ρ : Lab n)
  → ((x y : Fin n) → compat¹ (rel Ψ x y) (lab-at ρ x) (lab-at ρ y))
  → Mono Ψ ρ
Mono-mk {zero} Ψ ρ h = tt
Mono-mk {suc n} (Ψ , ρ₊) (ρ₀ , s) h
  = ( Mono-mk Ψ ρ₀ (λ x y → h (fsuc x) (fsuc y))
    , Compat-mk ρ₊ ρ₀ s (λ x → h (fsuc x) fzero) )

is-closed→
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) → is-closed Ψ
  → (x y z : Fin n) → compat¹ (rel Ψ x y) (rel Ψ x z) (rel Ψ y z)
is-closed→ Ψ cl x y z = Mono-at Ψ (col Ψ z) (cl z) x y

is-closed←
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n)
  → ((x y z : Fin n) → compat¹ (rel Ψ x y) (rel Ψ x z) (rel Ψ y z))
  → is-closed Ψ
is-closed← Ψ h z = Mono-mk Ψ (col Ψ z) (λ x y → h x y z)
}

\p{In the two-relation reading of \ref{ub-002S} that is exactly a
transitive relation, together with its reverse.}

\agda{
Le : ∀ {n} → Cvx n → Fin n → Fin n → Type
Le Ψ x y = le (rel Ψ x y)

Ge→Le : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (x y : Fin n) → ge (rel Ψ x y) → Le Ψ y x
Ge→Le Ψ x y p
  = ge-rev (rel Ψ y x) (tr ge (rel-rev Ψ x y) p)

Le→Ge : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (x y : Fin n) → Le Ψ y x → ge (rel Ψ x y)
Le→Ge Ψ x y p
  = tr ge (sym (rel-rev Ψ x y)) (ge-rev← (rel Ψ y x) p)

Le-trans
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) → is-closed Ψ
  → (x y z : Fin n) → Le Ψ x y → Le Ψ y z → Le Ψ x z
Le-trans Ψ cl x y z p q
  = ⊑-le (rel Ψ x z) (rel Ψ y z)
      (compat¹-le (rel Ψ x y) (rel Ψ x z) (rel Ψ y z) (is-closed→ Ψ cl x y z) p)
      q
}
}

\subtree[ub-002U]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{An extension of a closed presentation is closed}

\p{With [closedness read at a triple](ub-002T), the one-step formula is what
it looks like: the extended relation is #{x \preceq y} or
#{x \preceq a \land b \preceq y}, and that is transitive in four lines, one per
pair of cases. No fixpoint, no iteration, and no bound on how far to iterate.}

\agda{
rel-add
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (a b x y : Fin n)
  → rel (add Ψ a b) x y
    ＝ rel Ψ x y
      ⋎ (push (rel Ψ x a) (rel Ψ y b) ⋎ rev (push (rel Ψ y a) (rel Ψ x b)))
rel-add Ψ a b x y
  = ap (λ z → lab-at z x) (col-addC Ψ (col Ψ a) (col Ψ b) y)
  ∙ lab-at-addL (col Ψ y) (col Ψ a) (col Ψ b)
      (lab-at (col Ψ a) y) (lab-at (col Ψ b) y) x

module _ {n : ℕ} (Ψ : Cvx n) (a b : Fin n) where

  Le-add→
    : (x y : Fin n) → Le (add Ψ a b) x y
    → Le Ψ x y ⊎ (Le Ψ x a × Le Ψ b y)
  Le-add→ x y p
    = ⊎[ inl
       , (λ q → ⊎[ (λ r → inr ( le-push (rel Ψ x a) (rel Ψ y b) r .fst
                              , Ge→Le Ψ y b
                                  (le-push (rel Ψ x a) (rel Ψ y b) r .snd) ))
                 , (λ r → ¡ (ge-push (rel Ψ y a) (rel Ψ x b)
                               (le-rev (push (rel Ψ y a) (rel Ψ x b)) r)))
                 ] (le-⋎ (push (rel Ψ x a) (rel Ψ y b))
                         (rev (push (rel Ψ y a) (rel Ψ x b))) q))
       ] (le-⋎ (rel Ψ x y)
              (push (rel Ψ x a) (rel Ψ y b)
                ⋎ rev (push (rel Ψ y a) (rel Ψ x b)))
              (tr le (rel-add Ψ a b x y) p))

  Le-add←
    : (x y : Fin n) → Le Ψ x y ⊎ (Le Ψ x a × Le Ψ b y)
    → Le (add Ψ a b) x y
  Le-add← x y
    = ⊎[ (λ p → tr le (sym (rel-add Ψ a b x y))
                  (le-⋎₁ (rel Ψ x y) _ p))
       , (λ p → tr le (sym (rel-add Ψ a b x y))
                  (le-⋎₂ (rel Ψ x y) _
                    (le-⋎₁ (push (rel Ψ x a) (rel Ψ y b)) _
                      (le-push← (rel Ψ x a) (rel Ψ y b) (p .fst)
                        (Le→Ge Ψ y b (p .snd))))))
       ]

  Le-add-trans
    : is-closed Ψ → (x y z : Fin n)
    → Le (add Ψ a b) x y → Le (add Ψ a b) y z → Le (add Ψ a b) x z
  Le-add-trans cl x y z p q
    = Le-add← x z
        (⊎[ (λ p' → ⊎[ (λ q' → inl (Le-trans Ψ cl x y z p' q'))
                     , (λ q' → inr ( Le-trans Ψ cl x y a p' (q' .fst)
                                   , q' .snd ))
                     ] (Le-add→ y z q))
          , (λ p' → ⊎[ (λ q' → inr ( p' .fst
                                   , Le-trans Ψ cl b y z (p' .snd) q' ))
                     , (λ q' → inr ( p' .fst , q' .snd ))
                     ] (Le-add→ y z q))
          ] (Le-add→ x y p))
}

\agda{
add-is-closed
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) → is-closed Ψ → (a b : Fin n) → is-closed (add Ψ a b)
add-is-closed Ψ cl a b
  = is-closed← (add Ψ a b) λ x y z →
      compat¹-mk (rel Ψ' x y) (rel Ψ' x z) (rel Ψ' y z)
        (λ p → ⊑-mk (rel Ψ' x z) (rel Ψ' y z)
                 (λ g → Le→Ge Ψ' y z
                          (tr3 z x y (Ge→Le Ψ' x z g) p))
                 (λ l → tr3 x y z p l))
        (λ g → ⊑-mk (rel Ψ' y z) (rel Ψ' x z)
                 (λ g' → Le→Ge Ψ' x z
                           (tr3 z y x (Ge→Le Ψ' y z g') (Ge→Le Ψ' x y g)))
                 (λ l → tr3 y x z (Ge→Le Ψ' x y g) l))
  where
    Ψ' = add Ψ a b
    tr3 = Le-add-trans Ψ a b cl
}
}

\subtree[ub-002V]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Presentations from a list of relations, and their meets}

\p{With \ref{ub-002U} the class of convex pieces is concrete.
Name a convex subtype by a finite list of ordered pairs — the relations it
declares — and read it as the presentation got by adjoining them to
[the empty one](ub-002P) one at a time. Every such presentation is closed, its
locus is the conjunction of the listed inequalities, and the \em{meet} of two
names is the concatenation of their lists: no closure to compute, because
\ref{ub-002U} closes at every step.}

\agda{
Rels : ℕ → Type
Rels n = List (Fin n × Fin n)

adds : ∀ {n} → Cvx n → Rels n → Cvx n
adds Ψ [] = Ψ
adds Ψ ((a , b) ∷ L) = adds (add Ψ a b) L

adds-is-closed
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) → is-closed Ψ → (L : Rels n) → is-closed (adds Ψ L)
adds-is-closed Ψ cl [] = cl
adds-is-closed Ψ cl ((a , b) ∷ L)
  = adds-is-closed (add Ψ a b) (add-is-closed Ψ cl a b) L

Pres : ∀ {n} → Rels n → Cvx n
Pres L = adds discrete L

Pres-is-closed : ∀ {n} (L : Rels n) → is-closed (Pres L)
Pres-is-closed L = adds-is-closed discrete discrete-is-closed L
}

\p{What the list asks of a point, and that it is exactly what the presentation
asks.}

\agda{
Holds : ∀ {n} → Rels n → □^ n → Type 𝓘
Holds [] v = 𝟙ᴸ
Holds ((a , b) ∷ L) v = (□-coord v a ≤ □-coord v b) × Holds L v

Holds-is-prop : ∀ {n} (L : Rels n) (v : □^ n) → is-prop (Holds L v)
Holds-is-prop [] v = is-prop←is-single 𝟙ᴸ-is-singleton
Holds-is-prop ((a , b) ∷ L) v = ×-is-prop ≤-is-prop (Holds-is-prop L v)

adds-locus←
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (L : Rels n) (v : □^ n)
  → Locus Ψ v → Holds L v → Locus (adds Ψ L) v
adds-locus← Ψ [] v l h = l
adds-locus← Ψ ((a , b) ∷ L) v l (p , h)
  = adds-locus← (add Ψ a b) L v (add-sound Ψ a b v l p) h

adds-locus→₁
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (L : Rels n) (v : □^ n)
  → Locus (adds Ψ L) v → Locus Ψ v
adds-locus→₁ Ψ [] v l = l
adds-locus→₁ Ψ ((a , b) ∷ L) v l
  = addC-forget Ψ (col Ψ a) (col Ψ b) v
      (adds-locus→₁ (add Ψ a b) L v l)

adds-locus→₂
  : ∀ {n} (Ψ : Cvx n) (L : Rels n) (v : □^ n)
  → Locus (adds Ψ L) v → Holds L v
adds-locus→₂ Ψ [] v l = ttᴸ
adds-locus→₂ Ψ ((a , b) ∷ L) v l
  = ( add-≤ Ψ a b v (adds-locus→₁ (add Ψ a b) L v l)
    , adds-locus→₂ (add Ψ a b) L v l )

Pres-locus→ : ∀ {n} (L : Rels n) (v : □^ n) → Locus (Pres L) v → Holds L v
Pres-locus→ L v = adds-locus→₂ discrete L v

Pres-locus← : ∀ {n} (L : Rels n) (v : □^ n) → Holds L v → Locus (Pres L) v
Pres-locus← L v = adds-locus← discrete L v (Locus-discrete v)
}

\p{Concatenation is the meet, on the nose.}

\agda{
Holds-++→
  : ∀ {n} (L M : Rels n) (v : □^ n) → Holds (L ++ M) v → Holds L v × Holds M v
Holds-++→ [] M v h = (ttᴸ , h)
Holds-++→ ((a , b) ∷ L) M v (p , h)
  = let r = Holds-++→ L M v h in ((p , r .fst) , r .snd)

Holds-++←
  : ∀ {n} (L M : Rels n) (v : □^ n) → Holds L v → Holds M v → Holds (L ++ M) v
Holds-++← [] M v hl hm = hm
Holds-++← ((a , b) ∷ L) M v (p , hl) hm = (p , Holds-++← L M v hl hm)
}
}

\subtree[ub-002W]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Step 1 at a list presentation}

\p{Step 1 at every name of [the class](ub-002V): adjoining the comparability
of #{x_i} and #{x_j} to the locus of a list of relations is inner anodyne.
There are two cases and the list decides which: the presentation is closed,
so the slot it records at #{(j,i)} is the whole truth about that pair. If it
is #{\parallel} this is \ref{ub-002M}; otherwise the comparability already
holds on the locus and the inclusion is an equivalence.}

\agda{
private
  fam←slot
    : (s : Slot) → ¬ (s ＝ fr) → {x y : Δ¹} → bound¹ s x y
    → generating-fam-is-simplicial (y , x)
  fam←slot lo nf p = ι₂ p
  fam←slot eq nf p = ι₁ (≥←＝ p)
  fam←slot fr nf p = ¡ (nf refl)
  fam←slot hi nf p = ι₁ p

comparable-pair-is-inner-anodyne←related
  : ∀ {m} (Ψ : Cvx (suc m)) (i j : Fin (suc m))
  → ¬ (lab-at (col Ψ i) j ＝ fr)
  → is-inner-anodyne (fst {B = comparable-pair Ψ i j})
comparable-pair-is-inner-anodyne←related {m} Ψ i j nf
  = is-anodyne←is-equiv Λ[2,1]-incl
      (singleton-fst-is-equiv λ q → mk-singl (inhabited q)
        (generating-fam-is-simplicial-is-prop _ (inhabited q)))
  where
    inhabited : (q : Σ (□^ (suc m)) (Locus Ψ)) → comparable-pair Ψ i j q
    inhabited q
      = fam←slot (lab-at (col Ψ i) j) nf
          (Bound-at (col Ψ i) (q .fst) (□-coord (q .fst) i)
            (Bound-col Ψ i (q .fst) (q .snd)) j)
}

\agda{
module _ {m : ℕ} (Ψ : Cvx (suc m)) (cl : is-closed Ψ) (i j : Fin (suc m)) where

  private
    ¬fr : (s : Slot) → le s ⊎ ge s → ¬ (s ＝ fr)
    ¬fr s = ⊎[ (λ p q → tr le q p) , (λ p q → tr ge q p) ]

    related
      : ∀ {s} → ¬ (s ＝ fr) → lab-at (col Ψ i) j ＝ s
      → is-inner-anodyne (fst {B = comparable-pair Ψ i j})
    related nf e
      = comparable-pair-is-inner-anodyne←related Ψ i j (λ p → nf (sym e ∙ p))

    branch
      : (s : Slot) → lab-at (col Ψ i) j ＝ s
      → is-inner-anodyne (fst {B = comparable-pair Ψ i j})
    branch lo e = related (¬fr lo (inl tt)) e
    branch eq e = related (¬fr eq (inl tt)) e
    branch hi e = related (¬fr hi (inr tt)) e
    branch fr e = convex-pair-comparability-is-inner-anodyne Ψ cl i j e

  closed-pair-comparability-is-inner-anodyne
    : is-inner-anodyne (fst {B = comparable-pair Ψ i j})
  closed-pair-comparability-is-inner-anodyne
    = branch (lab-at (col Ψ i) j) refl

Rels-comparability-is-inner-anodyne
  : ∀ {m} (L : Rels (suc m)) (i j : Fin (suc m))
  → is-inner-anodyne (fst {B = comparable-pair (Pres L) i j})
Rels-comparability-is-inner-anodyne L i j
  = closed-pair-comparability-is-inner-anodyne (Pres L) (Pres-is-closed L) i j
}
}

\subtree[ub-002X]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Comparability of a list of pairs}

\p{The subtype of the cube on which each pair in a list is comparable. Step 3
of [the outline](Synthetic.Categories.ConvexSubcubes) is that this inclusion
is inner anodyne for every list; the ordered subcube is the case where the list
is every pair.}

\agda{
cmp-at : ∀ {n} → Fin n → Fin n → □^ n → Type 𝓘
cmp-at i j v = generating-fam-is-simplicial (□-coord v i , □-coord v j)

cmp-at-is-prop : ∀ {n} (i j : Fin n) (v : □^ n) → is-prop (cmp-at i j v)
cmp-at-is-prop i j v = generating-fam-is-simplicial-is-prop _

Cmp : ∀ {n} → Rels n → □^ n → Type 𝓘
Cmp [] v = 𝟙ᴸ
Cmp ((i , j) ∷ K) v = cmp-at i j v × Cmp K v

Cmp-is-prop : ∀ {n} (K : Rels n) (v : □^ n) → is-prop (Cmp K v)
Cmp-is-prop [] v = is-prop←is-single 𝟙ᴸ-is-singleton
Cmp-is-prop ((i , j) ∷ K) v
  = ×-is-prop (cmp-at-is-prop i j v) (Cmp-is-prop K v)
}

\p{[Step 1](ub-002W) again, in the shape [the glueing lemma](ub-000T)
speaks.}

\agda{
module _ {m : ℕ} (Ψ : Cvx (suc m)) (i j : Fin (suc m)) where

  private
    reassoc≃
      : Σ (□^ (suc m)) (∩-fam (cmp-at i j) (Locus Ψ))
      ≃ Σ (Σ (□^ (suc m)) (Locus Ψ)) (comparable-pair Ψ i j)
    reassoc≃
      = mk≃ (λ w → ((w .fst , w .snd .snd) , w .snd .fst))
          (is-equiv←qinv
            ( (λ w → (w .fst .fst , (w .snd , w .fst .snd)))
            , ~refl , ~refl ))

  ∩-comparability←pair
    : is-inner-anodyne (fst {B = comparable-pair Ψ i j})
    → is-inner-anodyne (∩-incl (cmp-at i j) (Locus Ψ))
  ∩-comparability←pair h
    = is-anodyne←equiv fst (∩-incl (cmp-at i j) (Locus Ψ))
        (mk-arr-equiv (reassoc≃ e⁻¹) (id≃: (Σ (□^ (suc m)) (Locus Ψ))) ~refl)
        Λ[2,1]-incl h

Rels-∩-comparability-is-inner-anodyne
  : ∀ {m} (L : Rels (suc m)) (i j : Fin (suc m))
  → is-inner-anodyne (∩-incl (cmp-at i j) (Locus (Pres L)))
Rels-∩-comparability-is-inner-anodyne L i j
  = ∩-comparability←pair (Pres L) i j
      (Rels-comparability-is-inner-anodyne L i j)
}
}

\subtree[ub-002Y]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Comparability of any list of pairs is inner anodyne}

\p{Steps 2 and 3 of [the outline](Synthetic.Categories.ConvexSubcubes),
together. The recursion on the list of pairs generates the disjunctive
presentation as it goes, so only [the binary glueing lemma](ub-000T) is used
and no #{2^{|I|}}-element family is ever written down.}

\p{The statement is relative to a convex base, and that is what makes the
recursion work: to adjoin the comparability of #{(i,j)} over a base #{L} one
splits into the two orientations #{L, i \le j} and #{L, j \le i}, which are
again bases, and glues. Their intersection is the base
#{L, i \le j, j \le i} — [concatenation is the meet](ub-002V) — so the third
input to the glueing lemma is the same recursive call again. The pairs still to
be dealt with never change; only the base grows. Writing #{A_L} for the locus
of #{L}, one step is the pushout of subtypes}

\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}
	{A_{L,\, i \le j,\, j \le i}} && {A_{L,\, j \le i}} \\
	\\
	{A_{L,\, i \le j}} && {A_L \wedge \square_{(i,j)}} && {A_L}
	\arrow[hook, from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[hook, from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[hook, from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[hook, from=3-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow[hook, from=3-3, to=3-5]
	\arrow["\lrcorner"{anchor=center, pos=0.125, rotate=180}, draw=none, from=3-3, to=1-1]
\end{tikzcd}
}

\p{(\ref{ub-0001}), followed by the inclusion that \ref{ub-002W} shows inner
anodyne. The glueing lemma is applied with #{T = \square_K}, the
comparability of the remaining pairs #{K}, to this square.}

\agda{
module _ {m : ℕ} where

  private
    Z : Type 𝓘
    Z = □^ (suc m)

    split
      : (L : Rels (suc m)) (a b : Fin (suc m)) (v : Z)
      → Locus (Pres (L ++ ((a , b) ∷ []))) v
      → Locus (Pres L) v × (□-coord v a ≤ □-coord v b)
    split L a b v l
      = let h = Holds-++→ L _ v (Pres-locus→ _ v l)
        in (Pres-locus← L v (h .fst) , h .snd .fst)

    merge
      : (L : Rels (suc m)) (a b : Fin (suc m)) (v : Z)
      → Locus (Pres L) v → □-coord v a ≤ □-coord v b
      → Locus (Pres (L ++ ((a , b) ∷ []))) v
    merge L a b v l p
      = Pres-locus← _ v
          (Holds-++← L _ v (Pres-locus→ L v l)
            (p , ttᴸ))

    split2
      : (L : Rels (suc m)) (a b : Fin (suc m)) (v : Z)
      → Locus (Pres (L ++ ((a , b) ∷ (b , a) ∷ []))) v
      → Locus (Pres L) v
        × ((□-coord v a ≤ □-coord v b) × (□-coord v b ≤ □-coord v a))
    split2 L a b v l
      = let h = Holds-++→ L _ v (Pres-locus→ _ v l)
        in ( Pres-locus← L v (h .fst)
           , (h .snd .fst , h .snd .snd .fst) )

    merge2
      : (L : Rels (suc m)) (a b : Fin (suc m)) (v : Z)
      → Locus (Pres L) v
      → □-coord v a ≤ □-coord v b → □-coord v b ≤ □-coord v a
      → Locus (Pres (L ++ ((a , b) ∷ (b , a) ∷ []))) v
    merge2 L a b v l p q
      = Pres-locus← _ v
          (Holds-++← L _ v (Pres-locus→ L v l)
            (p , (q , ttᴸ)))
}

\p{The two logical identities the transfers run on: the base together with a
comparability is the union of the two orientations, and the intersection of the
two orientations is the base with both.}

\agda{
  private
    U→
      : (L : Rels (suc m)) (i j : Fin (suc m)) (z : Z)
      → ∪-fam (Locus (Pres (L ++ ((i , j) ∷ []))))
              (Locus (Pres (L ++ ((j , i) ∷ [])))) z
      → ∩-fam (cmp-at i j) (Locus (Pres L)) z
    U→ L i j z
      = *-prop-rec
          (×-is-prop (cmp-at-is-prop i j z) (Locus-is-prop (Pres L) z))
          (λ x → (ι₁ (split L i j z x .snd) , split L i j z x .fst))
          (λ y → (ι₂ (split L j i z y .snd) , split L j i z y .fst))

    U←
      : (L : Rels (suc m)) (i j : Fin (suc m)) (z : Z)
      → ∩-fam (cmp-at i j) (Locus (Pres L)) z
      → ∪-fam (Locus (Pres (L ++ ((i , j) ∷ []))))
              (Locus (Pres (L ++ ((j , i) ∷ [])))) z
    U← L i j z (c , l)
      = *-prop-rec
          (*-is-prop (Locus-is-prop _ z) (Locus-is-prop _ z))
          (λ p → ι₁ (merge L i j z l p))
          (λ q → ι₂ (merge L j i z l q))
          c

    XY←
      : (L : Rels (suc m)) (i j : Fin (suc m)) (z : Z)
      → Locus (Pres (L ++ ((i , j) ∷ (j , i) ∷ []))) z
      → ∩-fam (Locus (Pres (L ++ ((i , j) ∷ []))))
              (Locus (Pres (L ++ ((j , i) ∷ [])))) z
    XY← L i j z l
      = let s = split2 L i j z l
        in ( merge L i j z (s .fst) (s .snd .fst)
           , merge L j i z (s .fst) (s .snd .snd) )

    XY→
      : (L : Rels (suc m)) (i j : Fin (suc m)) (z : Z)
      → ∩-fam (Locus (Pres (L ++ ((i , j) ∷ []))))
              (Locus (Pres (L ++ ((j , i) ∷ [])))) z
      → Locus (Pres (L ++ ((i , j) ∷ (j , i) ∷ []))) z
    XY→ L i j z (x , y)
      = let s = split L i j z x
        in merge2 L i j z (s .fst) (s .snd) (split L j i z y .snd)
}

\p{The recursion.}

\agda{
  route
    : (K L : Rels (suc m))
    → is-inner-anodyne (∩-incl (Cmp K) (Locus (Pres L)))
  route [] L
    = ∩-incl-is-anodyne←implies Λ[2,1]-incl (Cmp []) (Locus (Pres L))
        (Cmp-is-prop []) (Locus-is-prop (Pres L))
        (λ z _ → ttᴸ)
  route ((i , j) ∷ K) L
    = sub-anodyne-transfer-∩ Λ[2,1]-incl (Cmp ((i , j) ∷ K)) (Locus (Pres L))
        (λ z → ×-is-prop (Cmp-is-prop K z)
                 (×-is-prop (cmp-at-is-prop i j z)
                            (Locus-is-prop (Pres L) z)))
        (Cmp-is-prop ((i , j) ∷ K))
        (Locus-is-prop (Pres L))
        ( ∩-incl (cmp-at i j) (Locus (Pres L))
        ∘ ∩-incl (Cmp K) (∩-fam (cmp-at i j) (Locus (Pres L))) )
        (λ _ → refl)
        (λ z u → ((u .snd .fst , u .fst) , u .snd .snd))
        (λ z u → (u .fst .snd , (u .fst .fst , u .snd)))
        (is-anodyne-∘ Λ[2,1]-incl
          (Rels-∩-comparability-is-inner-anodyne L i j)
          (sub-anodyne-transfer Λ[2,1]-incl
            (λ z → ×-is-prop (Cmp-is-prop K z)
                     (*-is-prop (Locus-is-prop _ z) (Locus-is-prop _ z)))
            (λ z → *-is-prop (Locus-is-prop _ z) (Locus-is-prop _ z))
            (λ z → ×-is-prop (Cmp-is-prop K z)
                     (×-is-prop (cmp-at-is-prop i j z)
                                (Locus-is-prop (Pres L) z)))
            (λ z → ×-is-prop (cmp-at-is-prop i j z)
                             (Locus-is-prop (Pres L) z))
            (∩-incl (Cmp K)
              (∪-fam (Locus (Pres (L ++ ((i , j) ∷ []))))
                     (Locus (Pres (L ++ ((j , i) ∷ []))))))
            (λ _ → refl)
            (λ z u → u .snd)
            (λ z u → (u .fst , U→ L i j z (u .snd)))
            (λ z u → (u .fst , U← L i j z (u .snd)))
            (U→ L i j)
            (U← L i j)
            (glue-union.glue-union-is-anodyne Λ[2,1]-incl
              (Cmp K)
              (Locus (Pres (L ++ ((i , j) ∷ []))))
              (Locus (Pres (L ++ ((j , i) ∷ []))))
              (Cmp-is-prop K) (Locus-is-prop _) (Locus-is-prop _)
              (route K (L ++ ((i , j) ∷ [])))
              (route K (L ++ ((j , i) ∷ [])))
              (sub-anodyne-transfer Λ[2,1]-incl
                (λ z → ×-is-prop (Cmp-is-prop K z) (Locus-is-prop _ z))
                (Locus-is-prop _)
                (λ z → ×-is-prop (Cmp-is-prop K z)
                         (×-is-prop (Locus-is-prop _ z) (Locus-is-prop _ z)))
                (λ z → ×-is-prop (Locus-is-prop _ z) (Locus-is-prop _ z))
                (∩-incl (Cmp K)
                  (Locus (Pres (L ++ ((i , j) ∷ (j , i) ∷ [])))))
                (λ _ → refl)
                (λ z u → u .snd)
                (λ z u → (u .fst , XY← L i j z (u .snd)))
                (λ z u → (u .fst , XY→ L i j z (u .snd)))
                (XY← L i j)
                (XY→ L i j)
                (route K (L ++ ((i , j) ∷ (j , i) ∷ [])))))))
}

\p{Taking the base to be empty gives the statement over the whole cube. The
empty presentation constrains nothing (\ref{ub-002P}), so its locus is a
singleton family and the inclusion of it into the cube is an equivalence.}

\agda{
  Cmp-is-inner-anodyne
    : (K : Rels (suc m)) → is-inner-anodyne (fst {B = Cmp K})
  Cmp-is-inner-anodyne K
    = sub-anodyne-transfer-full Λ[2,1]-incl
        (λ z → ×-is-prop (Cmp-is-prop K z) (Locus-is-prop discrete z))
        (Cmp-is-prop K)
        (fst ∘ ∩-incl (Cmp K) (Locus (discrete {suc m})))
        (λ _ → refl)
        (λ z u → u .fst)
        (λ z u → (u , Locus-discrete z))
        (is-anodyne-∘ Λ[2,1]-incl
          (is-anodyne←is-equiv Λ[2,1]-incl
            (singleton-fst-is-equiv λ z →
              mk-singl (Locus-discrete z) (Locus-is-prop discrete z _)))
          (route K []))
}
}

\subtree[ub-002Z]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The ordered subcube, as a list of every pair}

\p{[The theorem above](ub-002Y) is about a list, and the ordered subcube is
about a quantifier. The passage between them is an enumeration, and this is
the only place one is needed: a list indexed by #{\Fin k}, and a grid of two of
them.}

\agda{
enum : ∀ {n} (k : ℕ) → (Fin k → Fin n × Fin n) → Rels n
enum zero f = []
enum (suc k) f = f fzero ∷ enum k (f ∘ fsuc)

grid
  : ∀ {n} (k : ℕ) (f : Fin k → Fin n) (l : ℕ) (g : Fin l → Fin n) → Rels n
grid zero f l g = []
grid (suc k) f l g
  = enum l (λ b → (f fzero , g b)) ++ grid k (f ∘ fsuc) l g

all-pairs : (n : ℕ) → Rels n
all-pairs n = grid n id n id

Everywhere : ∀ {n} → □^ n → Type 𝓘
Everywhere {n} v = (i j : Fin n) → cmp-at i j v

Everywhere-is-prop : ∀ {n} (v : □^ n) → is-prop (Everywhere v)
Everywhere-is-prop v
  = Π-is-prop (λ i → Π-is-prop (λ j → cmp-at-is-prop i j v))
}

\agda{
private
  Cmp-++→
    : ∀ {n} (K M : Rels n) (v : □^ n) → Cmp (K ++ M) v → Cmp K v × Cmp M v
  Cmp-++→ [] M v c = (ttᴸ , c)
  Cmp-++→ ((i , j) ∷ K) M v (c , cs)
    = let r = Cmp-++→ K M v cs in ((c , r .fst) , r .snd)

  Cmp-++←
    : ∀ {n} (K M : Rels n) (v : □^ n) → Cmp K v → Cmp M v → Cmp (K ++ M) v
  Cmp-++← [] M v ck cm = cm
  Cmp-++← ((i , j) ∷ K) M v (c , ck) cm = (c , Cmp-++← K M v ck cm)

  Cmp-enum→
    : ∀ {n} (k : ℕ) (f : Fin k → Fin n × Fin n) (v : □^ n)
    → Cmp (enum k f) v → (a : Fin k) → cmp-at (f a .fst) (f a .snd) v
  Cmp-enum→ (suc k) f v (c , cs) fzero = c
  Cmp-enum→ (suc k) f v (c , cs) (fsuc a) = Cmp-enum→ k (f ∘ fsuc) v cs a

  Cmp-enum←
    : ∀ {n} (k : ℕ) (f : Fin k → Fin n × Fin n) (v : □^ n)
    → ((a : Fin k) → cmp-at (f a .fst) (f a .snd) v) → Cmp (enum k f) v
  Cmp-enum← zero f v h = ttᴸ
  Cmp-enum← (suc k) f v h = (h fzero , Cmp-enum← k (f ∘ fsuc) v (h ∘ fsuc))

  Cmp-grid→
    : ∀ {n} (k : ℕ) (f : Fin k → Fin n) (l : ℕ) (g : Fin l → Fin n) (v : □^ n)
    → Cmp (grid k f l g) v → (a : Fin k) (b : Fin l) → cmp-at (f a) (g b) v
  Cmp-grid→ (suc k) f l g v c fzero
    = Cmp-enum→ l (λ b → (f fzero , g b)) v
        (Cmp-++→ (enum l (λ b → (f fzero , g b))) (grid k (f ∘ fsuc) l g) v c
          .fst)
  Cmp-grid→ (suc k) f l g v c (fsuc a)
    = Cmp-grid→ k (f ∘ fsuc) l g v
        (Cmp-++→ (enum l (λ b → (f fzero , g b))) (grid k (f ∘ fsuc) l g) v c
          .snd)
        a

  Cmp-grid←
    : ∀ {n} (k : ℕ) (f : Fin k → Fin n) (l : ℕ) (g : Fin l → Fin n) (v : □^ n)
    → ((a : Fin k) (b : Fin l) → cmp-at (f a) (g b) v) → Cmp (grid k f l g) v
  Cmp-grid← zero f l g v h = ttᴸ
  Cmp-grid← (suc k) f l g v h
    = Cmp-++← (enum l (λ b → (f fzero , g b))) (grid k (f ∘ fsuc) l g) v
        (Cmp-enum← l (λ b → (f fzero , g b)) v (h fzero))
        (Cmp-grid← k (f ∘ fsuc) l g v (h ∘ fsuc))

Everywhere→Cmp
  : ∀ {n} (v : □^ n) → Everywhere v → Cmp (all-pairs n) v
Everywhere→Cmp {n} v h = Cmp-grid← n id n id v h

Cmp→Everywhere
  : ∀ {n} (v : □^ n) → Cmp (all-pairs n) v → Everywhere v
Cmp→Everywhere {n} v c = Cmp-grid→ n id n id v c
}

\p{So the part of the cube on which \em{every} pair of coordinates is
comparable — which is the ordered subcube — includes inner anodynely.}

\agda{
Everywhere-is-inner-anodyne
  : ∀ {m} → is-inner-anodyne (fst {B = Everywhere {suc m}})
Everywhere-is-inner-anodyne {m}
  = sub-anodyne-transfer-full Λ[2,1]-incl
      (Cmp-is-prop (all-pairs (suc m)))
      Everywhere-is-prop
      (fst {B = Cmp (all-pairs (suc m))})
      (λ _ → refl)
      (λ z c → Cmp→Everywhere z c)
      (λ z h → Everywhere→Cmp z h)
      (Cmp-is-inner-anodyne (all-pairs (suc m)))
}
}
